add(31,'Functional Analysis',r'Which of the following is an inner product on the vector space of all real-valued continuous functions on $[0,1]$?', [r'$\left|\int_0^1 f(t)g(t)\,dt\right|$',r'$\int_0^1|f(t)g(t)|\,dt$',r'$f(0)g(0)+f(1)g(1)$',r'$\int_0^1f(t)g(t)\,dt$'],'D',[r'Test bilinearity and positive definiteness separately.',r'For the integral without absolute values, continuity makes a nonzero square positive on an interval.'],r'Option D is symmetric and bilinear. Its diagonal value is $\int_0^1 f^2\ge0$. If $f(t_0)\ne0$, continuity gives an interval of positive length on which $f^2$ is bounded below by a positive constant, so the integral is strictly positive. Hence it is an inner product. A and B fail homogeneity for negative scalars: with $f=g=1$, replacing $f$ by $-f$ leaves their value at 1 instead of changing it to $-1$. C is bilinear but not positive definite: $f(t)=t(1-t)$ is nonzero while its two endpoint values vanish.',r'A real inner product is a symmetric bilinear form that is positive definite: $\langle f,f\rangle>0$ for every nonzero vector. Positivity alone is not sufficient; absolute values applied to a bilinear expression normally destroy linearity under negative scalars. On a space of continuous functions, integration against a strictly positive continuous weight gives an inner product because a nonzero continuous function remains nonzero on a set of positive length. Endpoint evaluation instead gives a positive semidefinite form with a large nullspace. The induced norm is the square root of the diagonal form, and Cauchy–Schwarz then bounds the mixed integral. The integral norm does not equal the uniform norm, and this continuous-function space need not be complete in that norm. Always identify the underlying vector space before deciding whether a diagonal form has a nontrivial kernel.',[(r'Is $\int_0^1 t f(t)g(t)\,dt$ an inner product on $C[0,1]$?',r'It is symmetric and bilinear. A nonzero continuous $f$ is nonzero on an interval containing some positive $t$, where $tf^2>0$. Its integral is positive, so yes.'),(r'Find the nullspace of $f(0)g(0)+f(1/2)g(1/2)$.',r'The diagonal value is $f(0)^2+f(1/2)^2$. It vanishes exactly when both evaluations vanish. Thus the nullspace consists of all continuous functions with $f(0)=f(1/2)=0$, including $f(t)=t(t-1/2)$.')])

add(32,'Linear Algebra',r'Define $A\in M_n(\mathbb R)$ to be positive when $\langle Av,v\rangle\ge0$ for every $v\in\mathbb R^n$. Let $A_{\alpha,\beta}=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&1&1\\1&0&0\\1&0&\beta\end{pmatrix}$ and $S=\{(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathbb R^2:A_{\alpha,\beta}\text{ is positive}\}$. Which statement is true?',[r'$S$ is empty',r'$(\alpha,\beta)\in S$ if and only if $\alpha\beta>0$',r'$(\alpha,\beta)\in S$ if and only if $\alpha+\beta+4>0$',r'$S=\mathbb R^2$'],'A',[r'Inspect the principal submatrix on the first two coordinates.',r'Test $v=(1,t,0)^T$ and vary $t$.'],r'For $v=(1,t,0)^T$, $v^TA_{\alpha,\beta}v=\alpha+2t$. For every fixed $\alpha$, choosing $t<-\alpha/2$ makes this negative, independently of $\beta$. Thus no parameter pair belongs to $S$, and A is true. B and C each admit many pairs but none can actually be positive; D fails for the same reason. Equivalently, the principal $2\times2$ determinant is $\det\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}=-1$, incompatible with positive semidefiniteness.',r'A real symmetric matrix is positive semidefinite when its quadratic form is nonnegative on every real vector. This differs from positive definite, which requires strict positivity for every nonzero vector. Restricting the quadratic form to coordinate subspaces shows that every principal submatrix must also be positive semidefinite, and consequently every principal minor must be nonnegative. In particular, a zero diagonal entry forces every off-diagonal entry in the same row and column to vanish: otherwise a quadratic expression with a free linear term becomes negative for one choice of the second coordinate. For a symmetric matrix, eigenvalues are real and positive semidefiniteness is equivalent to all eigenvalues being nonnegative. Directly constructing a negative test vector is often faster than calculating eigenvalues or all minors. Conditions involving the full determinant alone are generally insufficient.',[(r'For which $a,b$ is $\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\b&0\end{pmatrix}$ positive semidefinite?',r'Its quadratic form is $ax^2+2bxy$. Taking $x=1$ and varying $y$ forces $b=0$; then nonnegativity forces $a\ge0$. These conditions also suffice.'),(r'Is $\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}$ positive definite?',r'Its quadratic form is $2x^2+2xy+2y^2=(x+y)^2+x^2+y^2>0$ for every nonzero pair. Therefore it is positive definite; its eigenvalues are 1 and 3.')])

add(33,'Complex Analysis',r'A function $f:\mathbb C\mapsto\mathbb C$ is said to be analytic at $\infty$ if $g(w)=f(1/w)$ is analytic at 0 after an appropriate value is assigned to $g(0)$. Which statement is true?',[r'Any nonconstant polynomial is analytic at $\infty$',r'If $f$ is analytic at $\infty$ then $f$ is bounded',r'For any $z_0\in\mathbb C$, the function $f(z)=e^{1/z-z_0}$ is analytic at $\infty$',r'Any entire function can be extended to an analytic function at $\infty$'],'C',[r'Use the coordinate $w=1/z$ to move infinity to zero.',r'For option C, substitution produces an exponential analytic at $w=0$.'],r'Under the printed exponent $1/z-z_0$, option C gives $g(w)=e^{w-z_0}$, which is entire and has $g(0)=e^{-z_0}$. Thus its germ near infinity is analytic. A is false: a degree-$m>0$ polynomial becomes a function with a pole of order $m$ at zero. D is false, for example for $f(z)=z$. B would be true for an entire $f$ by Liouville, but the premise states only analyticity near infinity. A function can equal zero for $|z|>2$ and take unbounded values inside that disk, so analyticity at infinity alone does not imply global boundedness. C has a finite singularity at zero; the assertion concerns its behavior near infinity, and any assigned value at zero leaves that behavior unchanged.',r'The point at infinity on the Riemann sphere has local coordinate $w=1/z$. A function is analytic at infinity precisely when its expression in this coordinate extends holomorphically through $w=0$. Equivalently, it is holomorphic outside a sufficiently large disk and has a removable singularity in the inverted coordinate. Such a function is bounded near infinity, but global boundedness requires additional control on the finite plane. If it is also entire, compactness of each closed finite disk supplies that control, and Liouville implies that the function is constant. Nonconstant polynomials instead have poles at infinity; nonpolynomial entire functions have essential singularities there. A finite singularity does not prevent analyticity at infinity. When an elementary formula excludes a finite point, distinguish that domain issue from the local assertion at infinity.',[(r'Classify infinity for $f(z)=z^3+2z$.',r'Substitution gives $g(w)=w^{-3}+2w^{-1}$, so zero is a pole of order 3. Infinity is therefore a pole of order 3, not an analytic point.'),(r'Is $f(z)=1/(z-2)$ analytic at infinity?',r'Its inverted expression is $g(w)=w/(1-2w)$, holomorphic for $|w|<1/2$ and equal to zero at zero. Thus infinity is removable even though $f$ has a pole at the finite point 2.')],note=r'The image prints $e^{1/z-z_0}$. Option C is a local assertion at infinity; assigning a value at its finite singularity does not affect that assertion. No entire-function premise is added to option B.')

add(34,'Complex Analysis',r'Define $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$ by $f(z)=\bigl||z|^2-1\bigr|^2$. Which statement is true?',[r'$f$ is complex differentiable at all complex numbers except $z\in\{0,1\}$',r'$f$ is complex differentiable only at $z=0$',r'$f$ is complex differentiable only at $z=1$',r'$f$ is complex differentiable only for $z\in\{0,1\}$'],[],[r'Write $f=u+iv$ in Cartesian coordinates; here $v=0$.',r'Both partial derivatives of $u=(x^2+y^2-1)^2$ must vanish.'],r'The literal formula is $u(x,y)=(x^2+y^2-1)^2$, $v=0$. The Cauchy–Riemann conditions are $4x(x^2+y^2-1)=0$ and $4y(x^2+y^2-1)=0$. Since these partial derivatives are continuous, the conditions are necessary and sufficient at each point. They hold exactly at $z=0$ or $|z|=1$, and the derivative is zero there. Hence A, B, C and D are all false as printed: for example $z=i$ is differentiable but is absent from B–D, while $z=2$ is not differentiable and refutes A. If D were intended to read “$z=0$ or $|z|=1$”, that corrected option would be true. That correction is not made in the transcription or answer labels.',r'For a complex function $f=u+iv$ with continuous first partial derivatives near a point, complex differentiability at that point is equivalent to the Cauchy–Riemann equations $u_x=v_y$ and $u_y=-v_x$. A real-valued function has $v=0$, so these equations require both real partial derivatives of $u$ to vanish. Complex differentiability at isolated points or along a curve is possible without holomorphy on an open set. A nonconstant real-valued holomorphic function cannot exist on a connected open set, as follows from the Cauchy–Riemann equations or the open mapping theorem. Squared absolute values of real expressions simplify before differentiation. The most important diagnostic here is to solve the entire locus of the gradient equations; testing just the real points 0 and 1 misses other points on the unit circle.',[(r'Where is $f(z)=|z|^2$ complex differentiable?',r'Here $u=x^2+y^2$, so $u_x=2x$ and $u_y=2y$. Both vanish only at zero; continuous partials ensure differentiability there, with derivative zero.'),(r'Where is $f(z)=(|z|^2-4)^2$ complex differentiable?',r'The partials are $4x(x^2+y^2-4)$ and $4y(x^2+y^2-4)$. They simultaneously vanish at zero and on $|z|=2$, exactly the differentiability locus. The derivative is zero throughout that locus.')],status='source_issue',note=r'The exact source formula $\bigl||z|^2-1\bigr|^2$ has differentiability locus $\{0\}\cup\{|z|=1\}$. No printed option states this; option D lists only the two points $\{0,1\}$. All literal options are false. Conditional correction and full derivation are supplied.')

add(35,'Complex Analysis',r'Let $f,g$ be entire functions with $\lim_{z\to\infty}f(z)/z^n=\lim_{z\to\infty}g(z)/z^n=1$, for a fixed positive integer $n$. Which statement is true?',[r'$f=g$',r'$f-g$ is necessarily a polynomial of degree at most $n-1$',r'There exist such $f,g$ for which $f-g$ is a polynomial of degree $n$',r'There exist such $f,g$ for which $f-g$ is not a polynomial'],'B',[r'The limits give polynomial growth bounds on both entire functions.',r'Cauchy coefficient estimates eliminate degrees above $n$, and the leading coefficients agree.'],r'For sufficiently large $|z|$, $|f(z)|,|g(z)|\le2|z|^n$. Cauchy estimates on circles of radius $R$ imply that Taylor coefficients above degree $n$ vanish as $R\to\infty$. Thus $f,g$ are polynomials of degree at most $n$, and each limit forces its degree to be exactly $n$ with leading coefficient 1. Their leading terms cancel, so $f-g$ has degree at most $n-1$ (or is zero), proving B. A fails with $f=z^n$, $g=z^n+1$; C fails by leading-term cancellation; D fails because both functions are polynomials.',r'An entire function with polynomial growth is a polynomial. More precisely, if $|h(z)|\le C(1+|z|^m)$ globally for some integer $m\ge0$, Cauchy estimates give $|h^{(k)}(0)|/k!\le C(1+R^m)/R^k$ for every circle of radius $R$. Letting $R$ tend to infinity makes all coefficients with $k>m$ zero. A bound outside a disk can be extended to a global bound using the maximum of the continuous function on that disk. A complex limit at infinity is uniform over all directions outside sufficiently large disks, a stronger hypothesis than a limit on the positive real axis. After polynomiality is established, division by $z^n$ identifies the degree and leading coefficient. Equal leading coefficients yield cancellation, but do not imply equality of all lower-order terms.',[(r'If an entire $h$ satisfies $h(z)/z^2\to3$, describe $h$.',r'The limit bounds $h$ by a multiple of $|z|^2$ outside a disk. Cauchy estimates force degree at most 2, and the limit identifies the coefficient of $z^2$ as 3. Thus $h(z)=3z^2+az+b$.'),(r'If entire $h$ is $o(|z|)$ at infinity, what is $h$?',r'Cauchy estimates for $k\ge1$ give $|h^{(k)}(0)|/k!\le M(R)/R^k$ with $M(R)=o(R)$. Every positive-order coefficient vanishes, including $k=1$, so $h$ is constant.')])

add(36,'Complex Analysis',r'Let $f$ be holomorphic on $\{|z|<2\}$. Its only zero in $\{|z|\le1\}$ is a simple zero at the origin. For the positively oriented unit circle $\gamma$, find $\int_\gamma dz/f(z)$.',[r'$2\pi i f\prime(0)$',r'$2\pi i f\prime\prime(0)$',r'$2\pi i/f\prime(0)$',r'$2\pi i/f\prime\prime(0)$'],'C',[r'A simple zero of $f$ becomes a simple pole of $1/f$.',r'Factor $f(z)=zh(z)$ and evaluate the residue using $h(0)=f\prime(0)$.'],r'Simplicity gives $f(z)=zh(z)$ near zero, with $h$ holomorphic and $h(0)=f\prime(0)\ne0$. Therefore $\operatorname{Res}_{0}(1/f)=\lim_{z\to0}z/f(z)=1/f\prime(0)$. There are no other poles inside or on the circle, so the residue theorem gives $2\pi i/f\prime(0)$, option C. A reverses the reciprocal and is not generally equal. B and D use the second derivative, which does not determine this residue and may even vanish, as in $f(z)=z$. Positive orientation determines the plus sign.',r'The residue theorem states that the integral of a meromorphic function around a positively oriented simple closed contour is $2\pi i$ times the sum of residues of its interior poles, provided no singularity lies on the contour. If a holomorphic function has a simple zero at $a$, then its derivative at $a$ is nonzero and its reciprocal has a simple pole with residue $1/f\prime(a)$. This follows directly from the first-order Taylor expansion, or from the factorization $f(z)=(z-a)h(z)$ with $h(a)=f\prime(a)$. At a multiple zero, higher coefficients matter and the simple reciprocal formula is no longer applicable. Always check both the multiplicity of zeros and the absence of boundary zeros. Reversing the orientation changes the sign of the integral.',[(r'Compute $\oint_{|z|=1}dz/(2z+z^2)$.',r'The denominator has zeros 0 and $-2$. Only 0 is inside, and its derivative at zero is 2. The residue is $1/2$, so the positively oriented integral is $\pi i$.'),(r'Compute $\oint_{|z|=2}dz/[z(z-1)]$.',r'There are simple poles at 0 and 1. Their residues are $-1$ and 1, whose sum is zero. Hence the integral is zero, illustrating that each interior zero must be counted.')])

add(37,'Number Theory',r'The last two digits of $3^{2019}$ are',[r'27',r'37',r'57',r'67'],'D',[r'Work modulo 100.',r'Use $3^{20}\equiv1\pmod{100}$ and $2019\equiv19\pmod{20}$.'],r'Since $\gcd(3,100)=1$, Euler gives $3^{40}\equiv1\pmod{100}$; more sharply, $3^{10}=59049\equiv49$ and $3^{20}\equiv49^2\equiv1$. Thus $3^{2019}\equiv3^{19}\equiv3^{-1}\pmod{100}$. The inverse is 67 because $3\cdot67=201\equiv1\pmod{100}$. Hence the last two digits are 67, D; the other three proposed residues are distinct and incorrect.',r'Last digits are residues modulo a power of ten: the last two digits are determined modulo 100. When a base is coprime to the modulus, its powers form a periodic sequence in the multiplicative group of units. Euler gives an exponent dividing $\varphi(m)$, while direct computation can reveal a shorter period. Exponents should only be reduced modulo a verified period; reducing modulo 100 itself is not valid. A negative exponent denotes a multiplicative inverse and is legitimate only for a unit. The Chinese remainder theorem also allows computation modulo 4 and 25 and reconstruction modulo 100. For bases sharing factors with 100, Euler does not apply, and prime-power valuations must be examined separately. Leading zeroes should be retained when a last-two-digit residue is below ten.',[(r'Find the last two digits of $3^{40}$.',r'Since $3^{20}\equiv1\pmod{100}$, squaring gives $3^{40}\equiv1$. The last two digits are 01.'),(r'Find the last two digits of $7^{2020}$.',r'$7^2=49$ and $7^4=2401\equiv1\pmod{100}$. As 2020 is divisible by 4, the residue is 1 and the last two digits are 01.')])

add(38,'Abstract Algebra',r'Let $G$ be a group of order 2020. Which statement is necessarily true?',[r'$G$ is not simple',r'$G$ has exactly four proper subgroups',r'$G$ is cyclic',r'$G$ is abelian'],'A',[r'Factor 2020 and apply Sylow to the largest prime.',r'The number of Sylow 101-subgroups divides 20 and is congruent to 1 modulo 101.'],r'$2020=2^2\cdot5\cdot101$. The number $n_{101}$ of Sylow 101-subgroups divides 20 and satisfies $n_{101}\equiv1\pmod{101}$, forcing $n_{101}=1$. Its unique Sylow subgroup has order 101 and is normal, nontrivial and proper. Thus G is not simple, proving A. B is false for cyclic $C_{2020}$, which has one subgroup per divisor and therefore 11 proper subgroups. C and D are not forced: $D_{10}\times C_{202}$ has order 2020 and is nonabelian, hence noncyclic, where $D_{10}$ denotes the dihedral group of order 10.',r'Sylow theorems describe subgroups whose orders are the maximal powers of a prime dividing a finite group order. If $p^a$ is that maximal power, Sylow $p$-subgroups exist, are conjugate, and their number divides the cofactor and is congruent to 1 modulo $p$. A unique Sylow subgroup is normal because conjugation cannot send it to a different subgroup. A simple group has no nontrivial proper normal subgroup, so proving uniqueness immediately disproves simplicity when the Sylow subgroup is proper. These numerical restrictions do not generally establish commutativity or cyclicity. To reject a universal claim about groups of a given order, construct a group of that order violating it; direct products often provide convenient counterexamples. Cyclic groups have exactly one subgroup for each positive divisor of their order.',[(r'Can a group of order 55 be simple?',r'$55=5\cdot11$. Its Sylow 11-subgroup count divides 5 and is 1 modulo 11, so it is 1. The normal subgroup of order 11 is proper and nontrivial; the group is not simple.'),(r'How many proper subgroups does a cyclic group of order 12 have?',r'The divisors are 1,2,3,4,6,12, with one subgroup of each order. Excluding the whole group of order 12 leaves five proper subgroups, including the trivial subgroup.')])

add(39,'Abstract Algebra',r'Which statement is NOT true?',[r'The polynomial ring $\mathbb Z[x]$ is a Principal Ideal Domain (PID)',r'The polynomial ring $\mathbb Q[x]$ is a Principal Ideal Domain (PID)',r'The polynomial ring $\mathbb Z[x]$ is a Unique Factorization Domain (UFD)',r'The polynomial ring $\mathbb Q[x]$ is a Unique Factorization Domain (UFD)'],'A',[r'Over a field, polynomial division gives a Euclidean domain.',r'In $\mathbb Z[x]$, inspect the ideal $(2,x)$.'],r'$\mathbb Q[x]$ is Euclidean by polynomial degree, hence a PID and a UFD, so B and D are true. Gauss lemma implies $\mathbb Z[x]$ is a UFD because $\mathbb Z$ is a UFD, giving C. However $(2,x)$ is not principal. A hypothetical generator divides both 2 and x, and thus must be a unit: a constant divisor of x in $\mathbb Z[x]$ is $\pm1$. But $(2,x)$ is proper, since evaluation at zero followed by reduction modulo 2 maps the quotient onto $\mathbb F_2$ and annihilates this ideal. Hence A is the false statement.',r'A principal ideal domain is an integral domain in which every ideal has a single generator. A unique factorization domain requires every nonzero nonunit to factor into irreducibles uniquely up to order and multiplication by units. Every Euclidean domain is a PID, and every PID is a UFD; the converses need not hold. Polynomial rings over fields are Euclidean using degree because division by a nonzero leading coefficient is possible. Gauss lemma extends unique factorization from a UFD to its polynomial ring, but does not extend the PID property. A useful obstruction is an ideal generated by a nonunit integer and an indeterminate. To show it is not principal, combine divisibility of any hypothetical generator with properness of the ideal, often witnessed by a homomorphism to a finite field.',[(r'Why is $(3,x)$ not principal in $\mathbb Z[x]$?',r'A generator would divide both 3 and x. It must be a constant unit, so its ideal would be the whole ring. Yet evaluation at zero modulo 3 annihilates $(3,x)$ and sends 1 to 1, proving properness and contradiction.'),(r'Is $\mathbb R[x]$ a PID?',r'Yes. For a nonzero ideal choose a polynomial of least degree. Divide every other polynomial in the ideal by it; the remainder has smaller degree and lies in the ideal, so it vanishes. This polynomial generates the ideal.')])

add(40,'Topology',r'Suppose $f:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to(0,1)\times(0,1)$ is continuous and nonconstant. Which statement is NOT true in general?',[r'The image of $f$ is uncountable',r'The image of $f$ is path connected',r'The image of $f$ is compact',r'The image of $f$ has nonempty interior'],'D',[r'Continuous images preserve compactness and path connectedness.',r'Can the square map continuously onto a line segment lying inside the target square?'],r'The domain is compact and path connected. Its continuous image is therefore compact and path connected, proving B and C. Since $f$ is nonconstant, its image contains distinct points $a,b$. The continuous distance map $p\mapsto\|p-a\|$ on the connected image contains the entire interval $[0,\|b-a\|]$, so the image is uncountable, proving A. D fails for $f(x,y)=(1/4+x/2,1/2)$, whose image is a nondegenerate horizontal segment with empty interior in the plane. Thus D is the assertion not guaranteed.',r'Compactness is preserved under a continuous map: an open cover of the image pulls back to an open cover of the compact domain and hence has a finite subcover. Connectedness and path connectedness are likewise preserved, with a path transported by composition. A connected subset of Euclidean space containing two different points must be uncountable, as a continuous real-valued distance function has an interval in its range. Having nonempty interior is a separate dimensional property and is not preserved by continuous maps, even when the domain has nonempty interior. The image may collapse to a curve or segment. Nonconstancy rules out a singleton but does not prevent this collapse. Specify the ambient space when discussing interior: a segment has empty planar interior but nonempty relative interior in its supporting line.',[(r'Give a continuous nonconstant map from a disk with image of empty planar interior.',r'On the unit disk take $f(x,y)=(x,0)$. Its image is the segment $[-1,1]\times\{0\}$, which is compact and path connected but has empty interior in $\mathbb R^2$.'),(r'Can a connected set have exactly two points?',r'No in a Hausdorff metric space. If the points are $a\ne b$, disjoint sufficiently small balls separate them into two nonempty relatively open subsets. Thus the two-point set is disconnected.')])

add(41,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'Consider $t\dot y-3y=t^2y^{1/2}$, $y(1)=1$. Find $y(2)$.',[r'14',r'16',r'0',r'8'],'B',[r'On the positive solution branch set $u=\sqrt y$.',r'The resulting linear equation is $u\prime-3u/(2t)=t/2$.'],r'Let $y=u^2$ with $u>0$ near 1. Then $2tu u\prime-3u^2=t^2u$, so $u\prime-\frac{3}{2t}u=\frac t2$. Multiplying by $t^{-3/2}$ gives $(t^{-3/2}u)\prime=\frac12t^{-1/2}$. Hence $u=t^2+Ct^{3/2}$. The condition $u(1)=1$ gives $C=0$, so $y=t^4$ and $y(2)=16$, B. This branch stays positive for $t\in[1,2]$, making all divisions valid; direct substitution yields $t\dot y-3y=t^4=t^2\sqrt y$. The other numerical values do not satisfy the derived value.',r'Bernoulli equations have the form $y\prime+P(t)y=Q(t)y^r$ with $r\ne0,1$. On a branch where the required powers are defined and the solution is nonzero, the substitution $u=y^{1-r}$ turns them into linear first-order equations. An integrating factor $\exp(\int P)$ then permits direct integration. For a square-root equation, the real-valued unknown must be nonnegative, and $u=\sqrt y$ denotes the nonnegative branch; arbitrary negative solutions of the transformed linear equation cannot simply be squared and accepted. Dividing by the root also excludes zeros, so check whether the resulting branch reaches zero. Local uniqueness is standard where the right-hand side is continuously differentiable in y away from zero. Direct substitution and the initial value provide essential checks on the calculation.',[(r'Solve $y\prime=2\sqrt y$, $y(0)=1$, while $y>0$.',r'Put $u=\sqrt y$. Then $2uu\prime=2u$, hence $u\prime=1$ and $u=t+1$. Thus $y=(t+1)^2$ for $t>-1$; on that interval the positive-root branch agrees with $u$.'),(r'Solve $y\prime+y=\sqrt y$, $y(0)=1$, on its positive branch.',r'With $u=\sqrt y$, $2u\prime+u=1$. The integrating factor is $e^{t/2}$ and $u=1+Ce^{-t/2}$. Initial data force $C=0$, so $u=1$ and $y=1$.')])

add(42,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'For $\lambda\in\mathbb R$, consider $x_1\prime=x_1+2x_2+2x_3$, $x_2\prime=2x_1+x_3$, $x_3\prime=-x_3+2x_2+\lambda x_3$. If $\vec x(t)=\vec a e^{2t}$ (for some $\vec a$) solves the system, what is $\lambda$?',[r'2',r'4',r'6',r'1'],[],[r'A nonzero exponential solution requires 2 to be an eigenvalue of the coefficient matrix.',r'Use $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\2&0&1\\0&2&\lambda-1\end{pmatrix}$ exactly as printed.'],r'Literal substitution gives $(A-2I)\vec a=0$, with $A-2I=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\2&-2&1\\0&2&\lambda-3\end{pmatrix}$. Its determinant is $[-1][-2(\lambda-3)-2]-2[2(\lambda-3)]+2[4]=16-2\lambda$. If $\vec a\ne0$ is intended, this forces $\lambda=8$, absent from all four options. At $\lambda=8$, a valid eigenvector is $(3,-5,-2)^T$, as direct substitution confirms. In addition, the printed statement does not require $\vec a\ne0$: the zero vector solves the system for every $\lambda$, so its literal hypothesis imposes no restriction at all. With the conventional nonzero interpretation, the derived answer remains 8, not any listed choice. No alternative third equation is invented.',r'A constant-coefficient linear system $x\prime=Ax$ has an exponential solution $x(t)=ae^{rt}$ exactly when $Aa=ra$. For a nonzero vector a, this means r is an eigenvalue and the characteristic determinant $\det(A-rI)$ vanishes. The zero vector is always a solution and never imposes an eigenvalue condition, so a nontriviality premise is essential when inferring parameters. Once a parameter is obtained, an eigenvector and direct substitution check both the determinant arithmetic and the transcription. Several terms in one equation may involve the same coordinate and must be combined as printed before forming the matrix. When no listed option satisfies the necessary determinant condition, report the discrepancy explicitly instead of altering a coefficient to manufacture an available answer. Eigenvectors are defined only for nonzero vectors.',[(r'For $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&\lambda\end{pmatrix}$, when does a nonzero $ae^{3t}$ solve $x\prime=Ax$?',r'$\det(A-3I)=(-2)(\lambda-3)$ must vanish, so $\lambda=3$. Then $-2a_1+a_2=0$ and $(1,2)^T$ is an eigenvector, giving the required solution.'),(r'Does the solution $x(t)=0e^{5t}$ prove that 5 is an eigenvalue?',r'No. Both sides of $x\prime=Ax$ are zero for every matrix A. For example $A=I$ has only eigenvalue 1 but admits the zero solution; a nonzero coefficient vector is required.')],status='source_issue',note=r'The third equation in the exact image is $x_3\prime=-x_3+2x_2+\lambda x_3$. Nonzero exponential substitution gives $\lambda=8$, which is not listed. The source also omits $\vec a\ne0$, so literally all real parameters admit the zero solution. Both readings are fully derived.')

add(43,'Partial Differential Equations',r'Which function solves $u_x+x^2u_y=0$ with $u(x,0)=e^x$?',[r'$e^x$',r'$e^{(x^3+y)^{1/3}}$',r'$e^{(x^3-3y)^{1/3}}$',r'$(x^2y+1)e^x$'],[],[r'Characteristics satisfy $dy/dx=x^2$, giving invariant $x^3-3y$.',r'Check differentiability and the PDE at the initial point $(0,0)$, not just at nonzero characteristic labels.'],r'Formally $u=F(x^3-3y)$. Initial data require $F(s)=e^{s^{1/3}}$ (real cube root), producing C. On each region where $x^3-3y\ne0$, this function is smooth, $u_x=e^{s^{1/3}}x^2/s^{2/3}$ and $u_y=-e^{s^{1/3}}/s^{2/3}$, so the PDE holds. However it is not a classical solution across $s=0$. In fact no classical solution in a neighborhood of $(0,0)$ can satisfy the stated data: differentiating $u(x,0)=e^x$ gives $u_x(0,0)=1$, whereas the PDE at $(0,0)$ gives $u_x(0,0)=0$. A has residual $e^x$; B has residual $4x^2e^{(x^3+y)^{1/3}}/[3(x^3+y)^{2/3}]$ where defined; D has residual $(2xy+x^2y+1+x^4)e^x$. Thus none is a global classical solution. C is the intended local characteristic expression on domains avoiding the singular characteristic, and is recorded conditionally, without assuming an unstated exclusion.',r'The method of characteristics reduces a first-order transport PDE $a u_x+b u_y=0$ to curves along which u is constant. The curves obey $dy/dx=b/a$ where a is nonzero. Initial data on a curve determine a smooth local solution when that initial curve is noncharacteristic, meaning its tangent is not parallel to the transport vector field. Here the vector field is $(1,x^2)$ and the initial line has tangent $(1,0)$, so the initial curve is characteristic at zero. Compatibility must be checked there: the directional derivative specified by the data cannot contradict the PDE. A formal invariant formula may satisfy the equation away from a characteristic but lose differentiability on it. Real cube roots are continuous at zero but have unbounded derivatives there, so continuity alone does not establish a classical solution.',[(r'Solve $u_x+u_y=0$, $u(x,0)=e^x$.',r'The invariant is $x-y$, so $u=F(x-y)$. Initial data give $F(s)=e^s$ and $u=e^{x-y}$. Its derivatives cancel everywhere, and the initial line is noncharacteristic.'),(r'Can $u_x=0$ have a differentiable solution satisfying $u(x,0)=x$ near zero?',r'No. The initial data imply $u_x(x,0)=1$, while the PDE requires zero. This is incompatible characteristic initial data, just as the original equation becomes $u_x=0$ at the problematic initial point.')],status='source_issue',note=r'The printed problem does not exclude $(0,0)$ or specify a weaker solution concept. Its initial data force $u_x(0,0)=1$ but the PDE forces 0, so there is no classical solution there. Option C is valid only locally away from $x^3-3y=0$; full conditional derivation is supplied.')

add(44,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'Consider $x^2y\prime\prime-2x(x+1)y\prime+2(x+1)y=0$. If a nonzero polynomial solves it, what is its degree?',[r'1',r'2',r'3',r'4'],'A',[r'Compare the highest-degree terms of a polynomial solution.',r'The coefficient of $x^{m+1}$ is $2(1-m)$ times the leading coefficient.'],r'Let $y=a_mx^m+\cdots$ with $a_m\ne0$. The only contributions of degree $m+1$ are $-2x^2y\prime$ and $2xy$, with total coefficient $2(1-m)a_m$. Hence $m=1$. Writing $y=ax+b$, substitution leaves $2b(x+1)$, so $b=0$ and every nonzero polynomial solution is $y=ax$ with $a\ne0$. Thus A is correct and degrees 2,3,4 are impossible. As usual the zero solution is excluded when referring to the degree of a polynomial.',r'For a linear differential equation with polynomial coefficients, polynomial solutions can often be found by comparing the largest powers after substitution. If $y$ has degree m, its first and second derivatives have degrees at most $m-1$ and $m-2$; multiplication by a coefficient may raise these degrees. A coefficient multiplying the highest resulting power must vanish, yielding a necessary condition on m. This condition is not automatically sufficient: substitute a polynomial of the resulting degree to determine lower coefficients and check existence. Exceptional small degrees, especially constants, deserve separate attention because their derivatives vanish. The zero polynomial solves every homogeneous linear equation but has no ordinary degree and is not a candidate in a degree question. Balancing leading terms is typically faster than finding all nonpolynomial solutions of the equation.',[(r'Find all polynomial solutions of $xy\prime-3y=0$.',r'For $y=\sum a_kx^k$, the equation becomes $\sum(k-3)a_kx^k=0$. Thus only $a_3$ may be nonzero, and the polynomial solutions are $y=Cx^3$, including zero.'),(r'Find polynomial solutions of $y\prime\prime-y=0$.',r'A nonzero polynomial of degree m has a $-y$ term of degree m while $y\prime\prime$ has lower degree, so its leading term cannot cancel. Therefore only the zero polynomial solves the equation.')])

add(45,'Numerical Analysis',r'Newton–Raphson approximates $\sqrt\alpha$ for $\alpha>0$. For the error $e_n=x_n-\sqrt\alpha$, which recurrence holds?',[r'$e_{n+1}=\tfrac12(e_n+\alpha/e_n)$',r'$e_{n+1}=\tfrac12(e_n-\alpha/e_n)$',r'$e_{n+1}=\tfrac12\,e_n^2/(e_n+\sqrt\alpha)$',r'$e_{n+1}=e_n^2/(e_n+2\sqrt\alpha)$'],'C',[r'Apply Newton to $f(x)=x^2-\alpha$.',r'Subtract $s=\sqrt\alpha$ from $(x_n+\alpha/x_n)/2$ and factor $(x_n-s)^2$.'],r'Newton gives $x_{n+1}=\frac12(x_n+\alpha/x_n)$ when $x_n\ne0$. Let $s=\sqrt\alpha$. Then $x_{n+1}-s=[x_n^2+s^2-2sx_n]/(2x_n)=(x_n-s)^2/(2x_n)$. Since $x_n=e_n+s$, $e_{n+1}=e_n^2/[2(e_n+s)]$, exactly C. A and B incorrectly apply the iteration to the error itself. D has a different denominator; it does not coincide with the derived identity except in special accidental cases. Near the positive root, the coefficient tends to $1/(2s)$, demonstrating quadratic convergence.',r'Newton–Raphson for a scalar equation $f(x)=0$ uses $x_{n+1}=x_n-f(x_n)/f\prime(x_n)$ when the derivative is nonzero. For a simple root with sufficient smoothness and a sufficiently close initial value, the method converges quadratically: the next error is bounded by a constant times the square of the current error. An exact error identity is especially useful for the square-root iteration, because it also reveals the allowed denominator and the sign of subsequent errors. Positive starting values remain positive, and after one step the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality places the iterate at or above the positive root. Distinguish the iterate from its error when substituting. The recurrence is undefined if an iterate is zero; convergence guarantees and algebraic identities should always state the domain where the iteration itself is defined.',[(r'One Newton step for $\sqrt2$ from $x_0=1$ gives what?',r'$x_1=(1+2)/2=3/2$. The error is $3/2-\sqrt2=(1-\sqrt2)^2/2$, which agrees with the exact error formula using $x_0=1$.'),(r'For $\sqrt9$, start at $x_0=4$. Compute $x_1$ and $e_1$.',r'$x_1=(4+9/4)/2=25/8$. Hence $e_1=25/8-3=1/8$. The formula gives $e_0^2/(2x_0)=1/(8)$ since $e_0=1$.')])

add(46,'Calculus of Variations',r'The Euler equation for extremals of $J(y)=\int_0^5[y^2+x^2y\prime],dx$ defines which type of solution curve in the $(x,y)$-plane?',[r'Linear',r'Quadratic',r'Cubic',r'Trigonometric'],'A',[r'Compute the two derivatives of the integrand in the Euler–Lagrange equation.',r'$L_y=2y$ and $d(L_{y\prime})/dx=2x$.'],r'For $L(x,y,y\prime)=y^2+x^2y\prime$, the Euler–Lagrange equation is $L_y-\frac d{dx}L_{y\prime}=0$, so $2y-2x=0$. Hence $y=x$, a straight line, and A is correct. The other curve types do not describe this equation. Equivalently, integrating $x^2y\prime$ by parts leaves the endpoint term plus $\int_0^5(y^2-2xy)dx$, whose fixed-endpoint stationary curve is again $y=x$. The question concerns the Euler equation; existence of an admissible extremal also depends on whatever endpoint data are imposed.',r'For a functional $J[y]=\int_a^bL(x,y,y\prime)dx$, smooth extremals under variations vanishing at fixed endpoints satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equation $L_y-d(L_{y\prime})/dx=0$. This follows by differentiating the functional with respect to the variation parameter and integrating the derivative term by parts. If the integrand is linear in $y\prime$, the Euler equation may reduce to an algebraic relation rather than a second-order differential equation. Boundary terms vanish for fixed-endpoint variations but generate natural boundary conditions when endpoint values are free. Satisfying the Euler equation is a necessary stationarity condition, not by itself a universal sufficient condition for a minimum. Rewriting an integrand by adding or subtracting a total derivative changes boundary terms but preserves the interior Euler equation under fixed-endpoint variations.',[(r'Find the Euler equation for $\int_0^1[y^2+xy\prime]dx$.',r'$L_y=2y$ and $d(L_{y\prime})/dx=d(x)/dx=1$, giving $2y-1=0$. Thus the stationary interior curve is the constant $y=1/2$.'),(r'Find the Euler equation for $\int_0^1[(y\prime)^2+y^2]dx$.',r'$L_y=2y$ and $L_{y\prime}=2y\prime$, so $2y-2y\prime\prime=0$. Hence $y\prime\prime=y$ and the extremals are $Ae^x+Be^{-x}$ with constants determined by compatible endpoint data.')])

add(47,'Integral Equations',r'For continuous $f$ on $[0,1]$, consider $f^2(t)=1+2\int_0^t f(s)\,ds$ for every $t\in[0,1]$. Which statement is true?',[r'There is no solution',r'There is exactly one solution',r'There are exactly two solutions',r'There are more than two solutions'],'C',[r'At zero, $f(0)=1$ or $-1$.',r'On every interval where $f\ne0$, differentiating the equation gives $f\prime=1$.'],r'At $t=0$, $f(0)=\pm1$. On any interval where $f$ is nonzero, its square is continuously differentiable, so $f$ is differentiable and $2ff\prime=2f$ implies $f\prime=1$. The branch starting at 1 must be $f(t)=1+t$ until a first zero, but this expression has no zero on $[0,1]$, so it holds throughout. The branch starting at $-1$ must be $f(t)=t-1$ before its first zero, which can only be at $t=1$; continuity fixes its endpoint value as zero. Both expressions satisfy the integral identity directly: their squared values are $(1+t)^2$ and $(t-1)^2$. There is no interior zero at which a new branch could be attached. Exactly two solutions exist, C.',r'Nonlinear Volterra integral equations couple the current value to the integral over all previous times. A continuous integrand makes the integral continuously differentiable by the fundamental theorem of calculus. If the left-hand side is $f^2$, differentiability of $f$ follows locally only where $f\ne0$, since there one can take a smooth signed square root. Dividing the differentiated equation by f is therefore legitimate on nonzero intervals but may discard behavior at zeros. Initial values obtained by evaluating the integral equation at its lower endpoint select possible branches. First-zero arguments then determine whether and where branching can happen. On a longer interval, solutions may remain zero for a period after reaching zero and then restart, so the specified interval matters. Finally substitute every proposed branch into the original equation, not only its differentiated form.',[(r'Find continuous solutions of $f^2(t)=4+2\int_0^t f(s)ds$ on $[0,1]$.',r'$f(0)=\pm2$ and $f\prime=1$ where nonzero. The possible branches are $f=t+2$ and $f=t-2$, neither vanishing on this interval. Both substitute correctly, so there are exactly two.'),(r'Give three solutions of $f^2(t)=1+2\int_0^t f(s)ds$ on $[0,2]$.',r'One is $f=t+1$. Another is $f=t-1$ throughout. A third is $f=t-1$ for $0\le t\le1$ and zero for $1\le t\le2$. For the third, the integral at 1 equals $-1/2$, and afterward both sides remain zero. Thus extending the interval changes uniqueness behavior.')])

add(48,'Classical Mechanics',r'A body moves freely in a uniform gravitational field. Its trajectory lies on which curve in phase space?',[r'Straight line',r'Parabola',r'Hyperbola',r'Ellipse'],'B',[r'Phase space uses position and momentum, not two position coordinates.',r'Eliminate time between $\dot p=-mg$ and $\dot q=p/m$, or use energy conservation.'],r'Along the gravitational coordinate q, the Hamiltonian is $H=p^2/(2m)+mgq=E$. Hence $q=E/(mg)-p^2/(2m^2g)$, a parabola in the $(q,p)$ phase plane, giving B. The same follows from $p=p_0-mgt$ and $q=q_0+p_0t/m-gt^2/2$. A would apply to a force-free particle, whereas an ellipse describes a harmonic oscillator phase portrait and a hyperbola occurs for an inverted quadratic potential. For free motion with additional horizontal coordinates, those coordinates and momenta supplement this gravitational phase relation.',r'Phase space records positions and their conjugate momenta, so a phase trajectory differs from the ordinary spatial path. For one degree of freedom with mass m and potential V(q), the Hamiltonian is $p^2/(2m)+V(q)$. When it has no explicit time dependence, energy is conserved and trajectories lie on its level sets. A uniform nonzero gravitational field has a linear potential, yielding a parabolic relation between q and p. A quadratic stable potential instead produces ellipses, while a negative quadratic potential produces hyperbolas. Momentum and velocity differ only by the positive constant mass in this elementary setting, so using velocity gives the same curve type after rescaling an axis. Coordinate orientation changes the direction of the parabola but not its type. Always identify the plotted variables before applying a familiar spatial-trajectory formula.',[(r'Find the phase curve for a harmonic oscillator with energy E.',r'For $V(q)=kq^2/2$, conservation gives $p^2/(2m)+kq^2/2=E$. For $E>0$, divide by E to obtain an ellipse with semiaxes $\sqrt{2mE}$ in momentum and $\sqrt{2E/k}$ in position.'),(r'What is the phase curve of a force-free particle with nonzero momentum?',r'$\dot p=0$ gives $p=p_0$, while $q=q_0+p_0t/m$. Thus the phase trajectory is the horizontal line $p=p_0$ in the position–momentum plane.')])

add(49,'Probability',r'$X\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$. Given $X$, $Y\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,X)$; given $X,Y$, $Z\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(Y,1)$. Find $E(Z)$.',[r'$1/8$',r'$3/8$',r'$5/8$',r'$1/2$'],'C',[r'The mean of a uniform interval is its midpoint.',r'First find $E(Y)$, then average $E(Z\mid X,Y)=(Y+1)/2$.'],r'$E(Y\mid X)=X/2$, so $E(Y)=E(X)/2=1/4$. Next $E(Z\mid X,Y)=(Y+1)/2$, hence $E(Z)=\frac12(E(Y)+1)=5/8$. Thus C is correct and the other numerical alternatives are not. No independence between X and Y is assumed: the conditional intervals explicitly depend on earlier values, and the tower property handles that dependence.',r'The law of iterated expectation, or tower property, states $E[E(U\mid\mathcal G)]=E(U)$ for integrable U and a sub-sigma-field $\mathcal G$. It permits averaging conditional distributions without first deriving the complete joint density. A uniform variable on an interval $(a,b)$ has conditional mean $(a+b)/2$ and conditional variance $(b-a)^2/12$. When the endpoints are themselves random, these expressions become random conditional moments and must then be averaged. Conditioning on both X and Y is stronger than conditioning on Y alone; another use of the tower property may simplify the expression if it depends only on Y. All variables here are bounded, so integrability is automatic. A common error is to replace every conditional uniform distribution by Uniform(0,1), thereby losing the dependence generated by random endpoints.',[(r'Let $X\sim U(0,2)$ and $Y\mid X\sim U(0,X)$. Find $E(Y)$.',r'The conditional mean is $X/2$. Since $E(X)=1$, the tower property gives $E(Y)=1/2$.'),(r'Let $Y\sim U(0,1)$ and $Z\mid Y\sim U(Y,2)$. Find $E(Z)$.',r'$E(Z\mid Y)=(Y+2)/2$. Averaging with $E(Y)=1/2$ gives $E(Z)=(1/2+2)/2=5/4$.')])

add(50,'Probability Distributions',r'$X_0=0$ and $X_k\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(k,1/2)$ for $k\ge1$. Let $N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(1)$, with $X_k$ independent of N for every k, and put $Y=X_N$. Given $Y=3$, find $P(N=3\mid Y=3)$.',[r'$e^{-1}/6$',r'$e^{-1/2}$',r'$e^{-1/2}/6$',r'$e^{-1/2}/48$'],'B',[r'Condition on N to identify the Poisson-thinned distribution of Y.',r'Compute the numerator $P(N=3)P(X_3=3)$ and divide by $P(Y=3)$.'],r'Conditionally on $N=k$, $Y$ has Binomial$(k,1/2)$ distribution. Summing, $P(Y=3)=\sum_{k=3}^\infty e^{-1}\binom{k}{3}2^{-k}/k!=e^{-1}/48\sum_{j=0}^\infty(1/2)^j/j!=e^{-1/2}/48$. The numerator is $P(N=3,Y=3)=e^{-1}(1/3!)(1/2)^3=e^{-1}/48$. Their ratio is $e^{-1/2}$, B. The two numbers involving division by 6 or 48 confuse the joint or marginal probability with the posterior; A is just the prior mass $P(N=3)$. Independence among the different $X_k$ is unnecessary for this calculation.',r'Poisson thinning states that if a Poisson count with mean $\lambda$ is independently classified into successes with probability p and failures with probability $1-p$, then the success and failure counts are independent Poisson variables with means $\lambda p$ and $\lambda(1-p)$. The marginal result also follows solely from a binomial conditional distribution given the total count. Bayes formula computes posterior probabilities by dividing a joint mass by the conditioning event mass. In this setting, after observing y successes, the total is y plus an independent Poisson failure count, so $P(N=y\mid Y=y)=e^{-\lambda(1-p)}$. This is generally different from the prior mass at y. When a randomly indexed variable is used, independence of each indexed variable from the index ensures its conditional distribution is unchanged; mutual independence of the sequence is a separate assumption.',[(r'$N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(4)$ and $Y\mid N\sim\operatorname{Bin}(N,1/4)$. Find $P(N=2\mid Y=2)$.',r'Thinning gives failure count $N-Y\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(3)$ independent of Y. Thus given $Y=2$, $N=2$ means zero failures, with probability $e^{-3}$.'),(r'In the same model, find $E(N\mid Y=2)$.',r'Given two successes, the total is $2+F$ where $F\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(3)$. Its conditional expectation is therefore $2+3=5$.')])

add(51,'Stochastic Processes',r'Let $(N_t^1)$ and $(N_t^2)$ be independent Poisson processes of rates $a>b$. The source defines $M_t=\max\{N_t^1,N_t^2\}$ and again $M_t=\max\{N_t^1-N_t^2,0\}$. Which statement is true?',[r'$X$ is Poisson with intensity $a-b$',r'$X$ is a birth-and-death process with birth rate a and death rate b',r'$M$ is Poisson with intensity $\max(a,b)$',r'$M$ is a pure birth process with birth rate $a+b$'],[],[r'First distinguish the maximum of the two counts from the clipped difference.',r'Clipping an unconstrained difference does not create the reflected birth-and-death chain: it hides its negative depth.'],r'The literal source defines M twice and never defines X, so A and B are undefined. Even if the second M is conditionally renamed $X_t=\max\{N_t^1-N_t^2,0\}$, no listed assertion is correct. A fails because X can decrease, for example when the difference goes from 1 to 0. B fails because clipping loses negative depth: from hidden difference 0 an a-jump raises X, but from hidden difference $-1$ an a-jump leaves X at zero. More concretely, the first holding time of X at zero has survival expansion $1-at+\frac12a(a+b)t^2+O(t^3)$, rather than $e^{-at}=1-at+\frac12a^2t^2+O(t^3)$ required by birth rate a. For $M=\max(N^1,N^2)$, the first jump has rate $a+b$, disproving C since $\max(a,b)=a$. D fails because while one count leads, only jumps of the leading count increase M, so its rate is a or b, not their sum. Conditional repair to $X=N^1-N^2$ without clipping would make B true as a birth-and-death chain on $\mathbb Z$; replacing M by $N^1+N^2$ would give a Poisson process of rate $a+b$. Neither repair is silently adopted.',r'Independent Poisson processes have stationary independent increments and almost surely no simultaneous jumps. Their sum is Poisson with the sum of the rates. Their difference is a continuous-time nearest-neighbor Markov chain on the integers, with upward rate a and downward rate b, often called a bilateral birth-and-death process. Applying a many-to-one transformation to a Markov chain need not preserve the Markov property. In particular, clipping negative states at zero hides how many upward jumps are needed before positivity resumes. A reflected chain, which suppresses downward jumps at zero, is a different construction from clipping an unrestricted chain after it evolves. A count maximum is nondecreasing, but its growth depends on which component leads and on their gap; monotonicity does not make it a Poisson process or a Markov pure-birth chain with a constant rate.',[(r'What process is $N_t^1+N_t^2$ for independent rates 2 and 3?',r'Over disjoint intervals the sums are independent, and the sum of Poisson$(2h)$ and Poisson$(3h)$ increments is Poisson$(5h)$. Thus it is a Poisson process with rate 5.'),(r'What are the transition rates of $D_t=N_t^1-N_t^2$ for rates 2 and 1?',r'A jump of the first process sends d to $d+1$ at rate 2; a jump of the second sends d to $d-1$ at rate 1. Since independent increments depend only on the present difference, D is a Markov birth-and-death chain on all integers with those rates.')],status='source_issue',note=r'Exact image repeats the symbol $M_t$ for two different definitions and options A–B use undefined X. Under the natural conditional renaming of the clipped difference to X, every option is still false: clipped difference is not the reflected chain, and maximum is not sum. Full literal and conditional diagnosis is supplied.')

add(52,'Probability',r'$X_i$ are iid normal with mean 2 and variance 3. Independently, $N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(4)$. Put $Y=\sum_{i=1}^NX_i$ for $N\ge1$ and $Y=0$ for $N=0$. Find $\operatorname{Var}(Y)$.',[r'12',r'16',r'20',r'28'],'D',[r'Apply the law of total variance conditioning on N.',r'Use $E(Y\mid N)=2N$ and $\operatorname{Var}(Y\mid N)=3N$.'],r'Conditional on N, the independent sum has mean $2N$ and variance $3N$. The total variance formula gives $\operatorname{Var}(Y)=E(3N)+\operatorname{Var}(2N)=3\cdot4+4\cdot4=28$. D is correct. A counts only the variability of summands at fixed N; B counts only the variability of the conditional mean; C is neither the sum of the two contributions nor the correct variance. Normality is not needed for this moment identity, only independence and the stated finite moments.',r'For a random sum $S=\sum_{i=1}^NX_i$, where N is independent of iid summands with mean $\mu$ and variance $\sigma^2$, conditioning gives $E(S\mid N)=N\mu$ and $\operatorname{Var}(S\mid N)=N\sigma^2$. Therefore $E(S)=\mu E(N)$ and $\operatorname{Var}(S)=\sigma^2E(N)+\mu^2\operatorname{Var}(N)$. The first variance contribution reflects randomness within a fixed number of summands; the second reflects randomness in the number itself. When N is Poisson with mean $\lambda$, its mean and variance coincide, yielding $\operatorname{Var}(S)=\lambda E(X_1^2)$. The convention of an empty sum being zero makes the same conditional formulas valid at N=0. Ignoring variability of the conditional mean is a common error when the summand mean is nonzero.',[(r'$N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(2)$ and independent iid summands have mean 0 and variance 5. Find the sum variance.',r'The formula gives $5E(N)+0^2\operatorname{Var}(N)=5\cdot2=10$. Zero summand mean removes the second contribution.'),(r'$N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(3)$ and every summand equals 2. Find mean and variance of the sum.',r'The sum is exactly $2N$. Thus its mean is 6 and variance is $4\operatorname{Var}(N)=12$, agreeing with $3E(X_1^2)=3\cdot4$.')])

add(53,'Statistical Inference',r'$X_1,\ldots,X_n$ are iid $N(\theta,\sigma^2)$ with known $\sigma^2$. Let $\bar X=n^{-1}\sum X_i$. Which is the minimum-variance unbiased estimator of $e^{2\theta}$?',[r'$\exp(2\bar X-\sigma^2/n)$',r'$\exp(2\bar X-4\sigma^2/n)$',r'$\exp(2\bar X-2\sigma^2/n)$',r'$\exp(2n\bar X-2\sigma^2/n)$'],'C',[r'Use the moment-generating function of the normal sample mean.',r'$E(e^{2\bar X})=e^{2\theta+2\sigma^2/n}$, then apply completeness.'],r'$\bar X\sim N(\theta,\sigma^2/n)$, so $E(e^{t\bar X})=\exp(t\theta+t^2\sigma^2/(2n))$. At $t=2$ the extra factor is $e^{2\sigma^2/n}$. Thus C exactly removes it and is unbiased. The sample mean is complete sufficient for the unrestricted normal mean with known variance (a full one-parameter exponential family), so Lehmann–Scheffé makes this function of it the unique MVUE. A has mean $e^{2\theta+\sigma^2/n}$; B has mean $e^{2\theta-2\sigma^2/n}$; D generally estimates a different exponential with mean $e^{2n\theta+2n\sigma^2-2\sigma^2/n}$. Hence only C works for general n and $\theta$.',r'For a normal variable with mean m and variance v, its moment-generating function is $E(e^{tX})=\exp(tm+t^2v/2)$. Exponentiating an unbiased estimator of m does not give an unbiased estimator of its exponential; an explicit variance correction is required. In a normal location model with known positive variance and unrestricted real mean, the sample sum, equivalently the sample mean, is complete sufficient because the natural parameter space is an open interval in a full exponential family. Completeness means that a statistic-based function with zero expectation for every parameter must vanish almost surely. Lehmann–Scheffé states that an unbiased function of a complete sufficient statistic is the unique minimum-variance unbiased estimator. Finite exponential moments ensure the variance here exists. The exponent multiplier must be squared in the correction term, which explains the factor 2 for estimating $e^{2\theta}$.',[(r'Find the MVUE of $e^\theta$ in the same known-variance normal model.',r'$E(e^{\bar X})=e^{\theta+\sigma^2/(2n)}$. Therefore $e^{\bar X-\sigma^2/(2n)}$ is unbiased and is a function of the complete sufficient mean, hence is the MVUE.'),(r'Find an unbiased estimator of $\theta^2$ using $\bar X$.',r'$E(\bar X^2)=\theta^2+\sigma^2/n$. Hence $\bar X^2-\sigma^2/n$ is unbiased; completeness and sufficiency again make it the MVUE.')])

add(54,'Statistical Inference',r'$X$ has density $f_\theta(x)\propto x^{\theta-1}(1-x)^{\theta-1}$ for $0<x<1$, $\theta>0$. For testing $H_0:\theta=1$ versus $H_1:\theta>1$ with one observation, what form has a size-$\alpha$ UMP critical region?',[r'$(1-\alpha,1]$',r'$[0,\alpha)$',r'$((1-\alpha)/2,(1+\alpha)/2)$',r'$[0,\alpha/2)\cup(1-\alpha/2,1]$'],'C',[r'Under the null X is uniform.',r'For any $\theta>1$, the likelihood ratio is increasing in $x(1-x)$, which is largest at the center.'],r'Against a fixed $\theta>1$, the likelihood ratio to the null is $c_\theta[x(1-x)]^{\theta-1}$. It is largest when $x$ lies nearest $1/2$, so Neyman–Pearson rejects on a centered interval. Under $\theta=1$, the density is 1, so size $\alpha$ requires interval length $\alpha$, giving $((1-\alpha)/2,(1+\alpha)/2)$, C. The ordering is the same for every $\theta>1$, establishing uniform most power. A and B reject in one tail, whereas D rejects in both tails; these are lower-likelihood-ratio regions for the stated alternative. Open versus closed endpoints has no effect for a continuous distribution.',r'The Neyman–Pearson lemma selects observations with the largest likelihood ratio for a most powerful test of a simple null against a simple alternative at a prescribed size. If every parameter in a one-sided composite alternative induces the same ordering of a statistic, a common critical region is uniformly most powerful. The symmetric beta family here has natural statistic $\log[x(1-x)]$, and increasing the parameter concentrates mass near the midpoint. Under the uniform null, critical-region probability is simply its total length. Symmetry alone does not say whether to reject centrally or in the tails; the exponent sign in the likelihood ratio determines that direction. Thresholds should be calibrated under the null, not under the alternative. Continuous densities make boundary randomization unnecessary in this example.',[(r'For the same beta family, test $\theta=1$ against $0<\theta<1$. What is the UMP size-$\alpha$ region?',r'The exponent $\theta-1$ is negative, so the likelihood ratio is largest where $x(1-x)$ is smallest, near 0 and 1. Equal symmetric tails of total null length $\alpha$ give $(0,\alpha/2)\cup(1-\alpha/2,1)$.'),(r'For $X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$, test $\lambda=1$ against $\lambda>1$ using one observation at size $\alpha$.',r'The ratio is $\lambda e^{-(\lambda-1)x}$, decreasing in x for every alternative. Reject for $X<c$ with $1-e^{-c}=\alpha$, so $c=-\log(1-\alpha)$.')])

add(55,'Statistical Inference',r'$X_1,\ldots,X_n$ are iid $N(\theta,1)$ and the prior is $\theta\sim N(0,\sigma^2)$. Under squared-error loss, which is the Bayes estimator of $\theta$?',[r'$\bar X/(1+\sigma^2)$',r'$\bar X$',r'$n\bar X\sigma^2/(1+n\sigma^2)$',r'$n(\bar X+\sigma^2)/(1+n\sigma^2)$'],'C',[r'Squared-error loss is minimized by the posterior mean.',r'Add the likelihood precision n and prior precision $1/\sigma^2$.'],r'The posterior kernel is $\exp\{-\frac12[n(\theta-\bar X)^2+\theta^2/\sigma^2]\}$. Completing the square gives posterior variance $(n+1/\sigma^2)^{-1}$ and mean $n\bar X/(n+1/\sigma^2)=n\sigma^2\bar X/(1+n\sigma^2)$. This mean is the Bayes estimator, C. A has the wrong shrinkage factor and omits n; B would correspond to a limiting flat prior rather than the stated finite-variance prior; D adds an unjustified nonzero prior-location term. For zero prior variance, the degenerate limit of C is zero, consistent with known $\theta=0$.',r'Under squared-error loss, conditional posterior risk decomposes as $E[(d-\theta)^2\mid X]=(d-E[\theta\mid X])^2+\operatorname{Var}(\theta\mid X)$, so the posterior mean is optimal. A normal likelihood and normal prior are conjugate. Their precisions, defined as reciprocals of variances, add; the posterior mean is the precision-weighted average of the sample mean and prior mean. With observation variance one, the sample mean variance is $1/n$, giving likelihood precision n. A finite zero-centered prior shrinks the data estimate toward zero, with shrinkage diminishing as n increases. Posterior variance is smaller than each of the input variances. Distinguish prior variance from observation variance: confusing them reverses the weights and can produce an estimator that fails the large-sample or concentrated-prior limits.',[(r'One observation $X\sim N(\theta,1)$ with prior $N(2,1)$: find the posterior mean.',r'The likelihood and prior precisions are both 1, so their mean is $(X+2)/2$ and posterior variance is $1/2$. Under squared-error loss this mean is the Bayes estimator.'),(r'For n observations with variance 4 and prior $N(0,1)$, find the Bayes estimator.',r'The likelihood precision is $n/4$ and prior precision is 1. The posterior mean is $(n/4)\bar X/(1+n/4)=n\bar X/(n+4)$; posterior variance is $4/(n+4)$.')])

add(56,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'$X$ is a $p\times p$ Wishart random matrix with variance matrix $\Sigma$ and m degrees of freedom. $A_{ij}$ denotes an entry of A. Which statement is necessarily true?',[r'$cX_{12}$ is chi-square with $m-p+1$ degrees of freedom for some suitable c',r'$cX_{11}$ is chi-square with $m-p+1$ degrees of freedom for some suitable c',r'$Y=AX$ is Wishart with variance matrix $A\Sigma A^T$ and m degrees of freedom for every nonsingular A',r'A $k\times k$ submatrix of X is also Wishart for $k<p$'],[],[r'Use $X=\sum_{r=1}^mZ_rZ_r^T$ for normal vectors $Z_r$.',r'Wishart transformations require congruence $AXA^T$, and square submatrices must be principal.'],r'Literally D does not specify a principal submatrix, and arbitrary square submatrices need not be symmetric or Wishart. For example with $p=2,k=1,\Sigma=I$, the submatrix consisting of entry $X_{12}$ can be negative, unlike a one-dimensional Wishart variable. A fails for this same signed off-diagonal entry. B is false in general: $X_{11}/\Sigma_{11}\sim\chi_m^2$, and scaling cannot change its gamma shape to $(m-p+1)/2$ when $p>1$. C fails since AX need not even be symmetric; the valid transformation is $AXA^T$. If D is intended to mean a principal submatrix $X_{II}$ using the same row and column indices, then $X_{II}=\sum Z_{r,I}Z_{r,I}^T\sim W_k(\Sigma_{II},m)$ and D is the intended answer. The literal generic submatrix claim does not support a unique true choice, so no unconditional label is assigned.',r'A Wishart matrix with integer degrees of freedom m can be represented as the sum of m outer products of independent centered normal vectors with common covariance $\Sigma$. This gives a symmetric positive semidefinite random matrix. Its diagonal entries, after division by their respective variance entries, have chi-square distributions with m degrees of freedom. Off-diagonal entries are sums of products and can have either sign; they are not generally gamma or chi-square variables. Linear transformation of the underlying normal vectors induces a congruence transformation $AXA^T$, not left multiplication alone. Selecting the same subset of coordinates in every vector gives a principal submatrix that remains Wishart. Selecting unrelated row and column sets instead produces a general cross-product matrix, without symmetry or nonnegativity. The distinction between a principal submatrix and an arbitrary submatrix is therefore mathematically essential.',[(r'If $X\sim W_3(I,8)$, what is the distribution of $X_{11}$?',r'It is the sum of squares of eight independent standard normal first coordinates, so $X_{11}\sim\chi_8^2$, not $\chi_6^2$.'),(r'If $X\sim W_3(\Sigma,m)$, give the distribution of its top-left $2\times2$ block.',r'Select the first two coordinates of each normal vector in the outer-product representation. They are iid $N_2(0,\Sigma_{\{1,2\},\{1,2\}})$, so the block is $W_2(\Sigma_{\{1,2\},\{1,2\}},m)$.')],status='source_issue',note=r'Option C prints $AX$, not $AXA^T$. Option D prints only “$k\times k$ sub-matrix” without “principal”; arbitrary square submatrices do not remain Wishart. D is conditionally correct under the principal-submatrix interpretation, which is fully derived.')

add(57,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'In $y_i=\alpha+\beta x_i+\epsilon_i$ for $i=1,\ldots,50$, not all $x_i$ are equal. Which is an admissible leverage for observation 11?',[r'0',r'0.01',r'0.1',r'1.1'],'C',[r'Use the diagonal of the least-squares hat matrix with an intercept.',r'$h_{ii}=1/50+(x_i-\bar x)^2/\sum_j(x_j-\bar x)^2$ and $h_{ii}\le1$.'],r'The leverage formula gives $h_{11}\ge1/50=0.02$, excluding A and B. The hat matrix is an orthogonal projection, so its diagonal entries are at most 1, excluding D. The value 0.1 is attainable: take centered predictors $x_{11}=2$, one other value $-2$, two further values $\sqrt{21},-\sqrt{21}$, and all remaining values zero. Then the mean is zero and $\sum x_i^2=50$, yielding $h_{11}=0.02+4/50=0.1$. Thus C is admissible, and the rank assumption holds because these predictors are not all equal.',r'In ordinary least squares, the fitted response is $\hat y=Hy$ with hat matrix $H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T$ when the design matrix has full column rank. H is a symmetric idempotent matrix, hence an orthogonal projection; its diagonal entries, called leverages, lie between zero and one and sum to the model rank. In simple regression with an intercept, centering the predictor gives $h_{ii}=1/n+(x_i-\bar x)^2/S_{xx}$, where $S_{xx}>0$ when not all predictors are equal. Consequently the intercept imposes the sharper lower bound $1/n$. Large leverage measures an unusual predictor position, not necessarily an unusually large residual or an influential observation. To establish admissibility rigorously, check both the general bounds and, when needed, construct predictor values that attain the requested leverage.',[(r'For n=10 simple regression, what is the leverage of an observation with $x_i=\bar x$?',r'The squared deviation term is zero, so $h_{ii}=1/10=0.1$. This is the smallest possible leverage in the intercept model.'),(r'If n=20 and $S_{xx}=40$, find leverage when $x_i-\bar x=2$.',r'$h_{ii}=1/20+4/40=0.05+0.1=0.15$. The intercept and predictor-deviation contributions must both be included.')])

add(58,'Statistical Inference',r'Independent identically distributed $(X_i,Y_i)$ are uniform on the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(\theta,0)$ and $(\theta,0)$ as printed, where $\theta>0$. Which statistic is sufficient for $\theta$?',[r'$\max_iX_i+\max_iY_i$',r'$\max_i(X_i+Y_i)$',r'$\max_i|X_i-Y_i|$',r'$\max\{\max_iX_i,\max_iY_i\}$'],[],[r'Check whether the printed vertices determine a nondegenerate triangle.',r'If the repeated vertex were $(0,\theta)$, the support constraint would be $X_i+Y_i\le\theta$.'],r'The last two printed vertices are identical, so their convex hull is a line segment, not a positive-area triangle. A uniform planar density on that purported triangle cannot be normalized. Hence the stated two-dimensional uniform model is undefined and no literal sufficiency answer follows. If one conditionally corrects the repeated last vertex to $(0,\theta)$, the triangle has density $2/\theta^2$ on $x,y\ge0$, $x+y\le\theta$. The likelihood then factors as $(2/\theta^2)^n\mathbf1\{\theta\ge\max_i(X_i+Y_i)\}\prod_i\mathbf1\{X_i,Y_i\ge0\}$, proving B is sufficient under that correction. A and D can confound which coordinate maxima came from the same observation, while C does not capture the required sums. Alternatively, choosing a one-dimensional uniform law on the literal segment would be a different model, with all Y values zero and several listed statistics coinciding; that is not silently substituted for the planar triangle.',r'A statistic is sufficient when the conditional distribution of the sample given that statistic is free of the unknown parameter. The factorization theorem provides an equivalent likelihood criterion for dominated models: all parameter dependence must appear through the statistic and a parameter-dependent factor. For uniform distributions whose support depends on a scale parameter, the indicator of membership is essential and often produces an extreme-order statistic. On the right triangle $x,y\ge0$, $x+y\le\theta$, the largest observed coordinate sum determines whether every observation belongs to the support. Density normalization uses the area $\theta^2/2$. Degenerate vertex sets have zero planar area and require a different dominating measure and explicit model specification. Before applying the theorem, verify that the purported probability distribution itself exists and that the support geometry has been transcribed correctly.',[(r'For iid Uniform$(0,\theta)$ observations, find a sufficient statistic.',r'The likelihood is $\theta^{-n}\mathbf1\{\theta\ge X_{(n)}\}\prod_i\mathbf1\{X_i\ge0\}$. Factorization shows the sample maximum $X_{(n)}$ is sufficient.'),(r'For iid uniform points on $[0,\theta]^2$, find a sufficient statistic.',r'The joint likelihood is $\theta^{-2n}\mathbf1\{\theta\ge\max_i\max(X_i,Y_i)\}\prod_i\mathbf1\{X_i,Y_i\ge0\}$. Thus the overall coordinate maximum, rather than maximum coordinate sum, is sufficient.')],status='source_issue',note=r'The exact image lists $(\theta,0)$ twice among the three triangle vertices. The resulting set has zero area, so the planar uniform triangle model is malformed. The natural nondegenerate correction $(0,\theta)$ is derived only conditionally, yielding B.')

add(59,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'From N households, take samples of size $0<n<N$ to estimate the proportion with more than one earning member. Sample I uses SRS without replacement and sample II uses SRS with replacement. Let $p_i$ be sample proportions and $\sigma_i^2=\operatorname{Var}(p_i)$. Which statement is true?',[r'$p_1$ is unbiased but $p_2$ is not',r'$p_2$ is unbiased but $p_1$ is not',r'$\sigma_1^2<\sigma_2^2$',r'$\sigma_2^2<\sigma_1^2$'],[],[r'Both sampling schemes have unbiased sample proportions.',r'Derive the finite-population correction and check when its inequality is strict.'],r'Write the population proportion as P. Both sample proportions have expectation P, so A and B are false. With replacement, $\sigma_2^2=P(1-P)/n$. Without replacement, the hypergeometric variance gives $\sigma_1^2=[P(1-P)/n](N-n)/(N-1)$. Thus $\sigma_1^2\le\sigma_2^2$ always, and D is false. C is strict only if $n>1$ and $0<P<1$. The stated $0<n<N$ permits $n=1$, in which case the variances are identical. It also permits populations with P=0 or P=1, giving zero variances for either scheme. Consequently no listed statement is necessarily true under the literal hypotheses. Under the additional nondegeneracy assumptions $1<n<N$ and $0<P<1$, C is the intended answer.',r'Simple random sampling without replacement selects a uniformly random subset of fixed size; with replacement it selects independent uniform population members. The sample proportion of a binary characteristic is unbiased under both designs because each draw or inclusion has the same marginal population proportion. Without replacement, negatively correlated draws reduce variance by the finite-population correction $(N-n)/(N-1)$ when the variance is expressed using $P(1-P)$. This correction equals one at n=1, is less than one at n>1, and becomes zero at a full census. If every population unit has the same binary value, both variances are zero independently of sample size. Distinguish non-strict variance reduction from strict reduction, and inspect endpoint sample sizes and degenerate populations before endorsing a universal strict inequality.',[(r'N=100, n=10, P=1/2: compare the two variances.',r'With replacement the variance is $0.25/10=0.025$. Without replacement multiply by $90/99$, giving $1/44\approx0.022727$. This is strictly smaller because n>1 and the population is mixed.'),(r'N=10, n=1, P=0.3: compare variances.',r'Each design draws one uniform household, so each sample proportion is Bernoulli$(0.3)$. Both variances are $0.3\cdot0.7=0.21$; there is no strict reduction.')],status='source_issue',note=r'Only $0<n<N$ is printed. Strict inequality C fails for permitted n=1 and for homogeneous populations. Both proportions are unbiased; full variance derivation and conditional intended C are supplied.')

add(60,'Operations Research',r'Maximize $4x+5y$ subject to $2x+3y\le14$, $x+2y\le9$, $x+y\le6$, $x,y\ge0$. What is the optimal objective value?',[r'24',r'26',r'27',r'25'],'B',[r'A nonnegative combination of two constraints can bound the objective directly.',r'Write $4x+5y=(2x+3y)+2(x+y)$.'],r'For every feasible point, $4x+5y=(2x+3y)+2(x+y)\le14+2\cdot6=26$. Equality requires $2x+3y=14$ and $x+y=6$, giving $(x,y)=(4,2)$. It is feasible because $x+2y=8\le9$ and both coordinates are nonnegative. Thus the upper bound is achieved and the optimum is 26, B. A and D are attainable lower values but not maxima; C exceeds the proven global bound. This gives both a feasible solution and an optimality certificate without enumerating every vertex.',r'A linear program optimizes a linear function on an intersection of half-spaces. In a bounded nonempty feasible polytope, a linear objective attains its optimum at an extreme point, though an entire edge may be optimal when the objective is parallel to it. Another method proves a global bound by taking a nonnegative linear combination of inequality constraints whose left-hand side equals the objective. Any feasible point then satisfies that bound. If a feasible point attains it, optimality is established immediately; these coefficients are dual multipliers. Constraints assigned positive multipliers must be active at an attaining point, reflecting complementary slackness. A solution of selected boundary equations must still be tested against all remaining constraints and nonnegativity. A bound alone or a feasible point alone is not a complete optimality proof.',[(r'Maximize $x+y$ with $x+2y\le4$, $2x+y\le4$, $x,y\ge0$.',r'Adding the two constraints gives $3(x+y)\le8$. Their intersection is $x=y=4/3$, feasible and attaining $8/3$. Thus the optimum is $8/3$.'),(r'Maximize $3x+2y$ with $x+y\le5$, $x\le3$, $x,y\ge0$.',r'Write the objective as $2(x+y)+x\le10+3=13$. The feasible point $(3,2)$ attains 13, so it is optimal.')])

add(61,'Number Theory',r'Let $\mathbb N=\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$. Which functions from $\mathbb N\times\mathbb N$ to $\mathbb N$ are injective?',[r'$f_1(m,n)=2^m3^n$',r'$f_2(m,n)=mn+m+n$',r'$f_3(m,n)=m^2+n^3$',r'$f_4(m,n)=m^2n^3$'],'A',[r'Prime-factor uniqueness directly applies to one formula.',r'For the others exhibit two distinct input pairs with the same output.'],r'A is injective: equality $2^m3^n=2^{m\prime}3^{n\prime}$ forces equality of the exponents of the distinct primes 2 and 3. B is not injective because it is symmetric: $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$ both give 5. C is not injective: $(3,2)$ and $(4,1)$ both give $17$, since $3^2+2^3=9+8=17=16+1=4^2+1^3$. D is not injective because $(8,1)$ and $(1,4)$ both give 64.',r'An injective function maps different domain elements to different values; to prove it, start from equality of outputs and deduce equality of every input coordinate. To disprove it, a single collision suffices, but both input pairs must actually belong to the specified domain. Unique prime factorization encodes multiple positive integer coordinates as exponents of distinct fixed primes, and comparison of prime valuations recovers those coordinates. Symmetric formulas on ordered pairs frequently fail injectivity by interchanging unequal inputs. Sums of powers can also collide even when no symmetry is present, so arithmetic examples must be verified. Products of variable powers lose information because prime valuations from the two coordinates can compensate for each other. Avoid trial examples involving irrational roots in a natural-number domain, and independently evaluate both outputs of any purported collision.',[(r'Is $(m,n)\mapsto5^m7^n$ injective on positive integer pairs?',r'Yes. Equality of outputs forces equal 5-adic valuations and equal 7-adic valuations, recovering m and n uniquely.'),(r'Is $(m,n)\mapsto mn$ injective on positive integer pairs?',r'No. The distinct pairs $(1,6)$ and $(2,3)$ both map to 6, so the function fails injectivity.')])

add(62,'Real Analysis',r'Let $(x_n)$ be a sequence of positive real numbers. Which statements are true?',[r'If $(x_{2n})$ and $(x_{2n+1})$ converge, then $(x_n)$ converges',r'If $((-1)^nx_n)$ converges, then $(x_n)$ converges',r'If $\lim_{n\to\infty}x_{n+1}/x_n$ exists, then $(x_n^{1/n})$ is bounded',r'If $(x_n)$ is unbounded, then every subsequence is unbounded'],'BC',[r'The two parity subsequences need to share a limit for the full sequence to converge.',r'For B use absolute values; for C bound successive ratios by a finite constant eventually.'],r'A is false: $x_{2n}=1$, $x_{2n+1}=2$ gives two different limits. B is true because $x_n=|(-1)^nx_n|$, and absolute value preserves limits. Positivity also forces a signed limit to be zero by comparing the signs on the parity subsequences. C is true under the ordinary meaning of existence of a finite real limit: eventually $x_{n+1}/x_n\le M$ for some $M>0$, hence $x_n\le x_NM^{n-N}$ and $x_n^{1/n}$ is bounded, with finitely many early terms harmless. D is false: $x_{2n}=n$, $x_{2n+1}=1$ is unbounded but has a bounded odd subsequence. Thus B and C are correct. If an infinite extended ratio limit were allowed, C would require qualification; for example $x_n=n!$ has unbounded roots.',r'A sequence converges when all sufficiently late terms are uniformly near one limit. Convergence of separately indexed subsequences implies convergence of the full sequence only when their limits agree and together they cover its tail. Continuous functions, such as absolute value, preserve convergence. Positive sequences are particularly amenable to multiplicative bounds: an eventual finite upper bound M on successive ratios gives at most geometric growth, and taking nth roots then yields a bounded sequence. This is related to the ratio and root tests, but no series convergence claim is needed here. Unboundedness means that some arbitrarily large terms exist, not that every late term is large or that every subsequence is unbounded. When a limit is said to exist without mention of extended real values, the standard interpretation is a finite limit; distinguish that from divergence to infinity.',[(r'If even terms tend to 3 and odd terms tend to 3, does the whole sequence converge?',r'Yes. For any tolerance, choose bounds for each parity subsequence and then take an index beyond both. Every later term belongs to one parity and lies within the tolerance of 3.'),(r'Show that $x_{n+1}\le2x_n$ for positive $x_n$ makes nth roots bounded.',r'Induction gives $x_n\le x_1 2^{n-1}$. Thus $x_n^{1/n}\le x_1^{1/n}2^{1-1/n}\le2\max(1,x_1)$ for all n, an explicit finite bound.')])

add(63,'Real Analysis',r'Let $f(x)=e^x$ on $\mathbb R$. Which statements are correct?',[r'There exists $C>0$ with $|f(x)-1-x|\le Cx^2$ for every real x',r'There exists $C>0$ with $|f(x)-1-x-x^2/2|\le C|x|^3$ for $x\in[-1,1]$',r'There exists $C>0$ with $|f(x)-1-x-x^2/2-x^3/3!|\le Cx^4$ for every real x',r'There exists $C>0$ with $|f(x)-1-x-x^2/2-x^3/3!|\le Cx^4$ for $x\in[-1,1]$'],'BD',[r'Taylor remainder bounds use a supremum of the next derivative on the relevant interval.',r'On $[-1,1]$, every derivative of the exponential is at most e; globally exponential growth defeats any polynomial bound.'],r'Taylor theorem at zero gives the second-order remainder $e^\xi x^3/3!$ with $\xi$ between zero and x. On $[-1,1]$ its absolute value is at most $(e/6)|x|^3$, proving B. The third-order remainder is $e^\eta x^4/4!$, bounded by $(e/24)x^4$ on that interval, proving D. A and C fail as $x\to+\infty$: the exponential minus any fixed polynomial, divided by the proposed polynomial power, tends to infinity. Thus no fixed global C works, and the correct options are B and D. At x=0 all remainder inequalities hold with both sides zero.',r'Taylor theorem with Lagrange remainder expresses a smooth function near a base point as its Taylor polynomial of degree m plus $f^{(m+1)}(\xi)x^{m+1}/(m+1)!$ for an intermediate point. A uniform bound on the next derivative over a compact interval gives one constant valid for every point in that interval. This is an estimate of the remainder, not an assertion that the remainder is exactly one fixed multiple of that power. For the exponential, all derivatives equal the exponential itself, which is bounded on compact intervals and unbounded on the whole real line. Local big-O estimates therefore do not automatically extend globally. As x tends to positive infinity, exponential growth dominates any finite polynomial. Include absolute values for odd remainder powers and check the base point separately if dividing by a power of x.',[(r'Bound $|e^x-1|$ on $[-2,2]$ by a multiple of $|x|$.',r'Mean value theorem gives $e^x-1=e^\xi x$ with $|\xi|\le2$. Hence $|e^x-1|\le e^2|x|$ throughout the interval.'),(r'Prove $|\sin x-x|\le|x|^3/6$ for all real x.',r'Taylor theorem at zero through degree 2 uses the third derivative $-\cos\xi$, whose absolute value is at most 1 globally. The remainder is $-\cos\xi\,x^3/6$, yielding the global bound.')])

add(64,'Real Analysis',r'Which functions are uniformly continuous on $(0,1)$?',[r'$1/x$',r'$\sin(1/x)$',r'$x\sin(1/x)$',r'$\sin x/x$'],'CD',[r'A continuous extension to the closed interval gives uniform continuity.',r'C tends to zero at the left endpoint and D tends to one; A and B have bad paired sequences near zero.'],r'C extends continuously to [0,1] by value 0 at zero, because $|x\sin(1/x)|\le x$. D extends continuously by value 1, using $\sin x/x\to1$. The extensions are uniformly continuous by Heine–Cantor, hence so are their restrictions. A fails with $x_n=1/(n+1)$ and $y_n=1/(n+2)$: their distances vanish while reciprocal values differ by 1. B fails with $x_n=1/(2\pi n+\pi/2)$ and $y_n=1/(2\pi n+3\pi/2)$: distances vanish while sine values are 1 and $-1$. Thus C and D alone are uniformly continuous.',r'Uniform continuity requires one input-distance bound to work simultaneously at every point of the domain. On a compact interval, continuity implies uniform continuity by the Heine–Cantor theorem. A continuous function on an open bounded interval that extends continuously to its endpoints therefore inherits uniform continuity. Conversely, a uniformly continuous real-valued function carries Cauchy sequences to Cauchy sequences, and thus has finite endpoint limits on a bounded interval. To disprove uniform continuity directly, construct two sequences whose distances tend to zero but whose function-value distances stay bounded away from zero. Oscillation can destroy uniform continuity near an excluded endpoint, but an amplitude tending to zero can suppress that oscillation sufficiently for a continuous extension. An unbounded derivative is not itself a proof of failure; $x\sin(1/x)$ illustrates this pitfall.',[(r'Is $\sqrt x$ uniformly continuous on $(0,1)$?',r'Yes. It extends continuously to [0,1]. Also $|\sqrt x-\sqrt y|\le\sqrt{|x-y|}$, so choosing input tolerance $\varepsilon^2$ gives an explicit uniform bound.'),(r'Is $\log x$ uniformly continuous on $(0,1)$?',r'No. Take $x_n=e^{-n}$ and $y_n=e^{-(n+1)}$. Their distance tends to zero while their logarithms differ by 1, violating uniform continuity.')])

add(65,'Real Analysis',r'Let $0<y\le x$ and let n be a positive integer. Which inequalities hold in general?',[r'$ny^{n-1}(x-y)\le x^n-y^n$',r'$nx^{n-1}(x-y)\le x^n-y^n$',r'$ny^{n-1}(x-y)\ge x^n-y^n$',r'$nx^{n-1}(x-y)\ge x^n-y^n$'],'AD',[r'Integrate $nt^{n-1}$ from y to x.',r'The derivative is bounded between $ny^{n-1}$ and $nx^{n-1}$ on that interval.'],r'$x^n-y^n=\int_y^xnt^{n-1}dt$. Because $t^{n-1}$ is nondecreasing for positive t and integer $n\ge1$, this integral lies between $ny^{n-1}(x-y)$ and $nx^{n-1}(x-y)$. Thus A and D hold. B and C fail, for example at $n=2,x=2,y=1$: the difference is 3, the y-based bound is 2 and the x-based bound is 4. Equality holds in all expressions when x=y, and for n=1, but these special cases do not make B or C generally valid.',r'If a differentiable function has derivative bounded between m and M on an interval, the mean value theorem bounds its increment between $m(x-y)$ and $M(x-y)$ for x at least y. The same result follows by integrating the derivative when the fundamental theorem applies. For the power function $t^n$ on positive arguments, the derivative $nt^{n-1}$ is nondecreasing, giving endpoint derivative bounds. This is also the geometric property of convexity: a secant slope lies between the endpoint tangent slopes. Inequality directions must remain consistent with the nonnegative interval length $x-y$. Special equality cases do not establish a universal inequality; one counterexample with n>1 and x>y disproves a reversed bound. An alternative proof factors $x^n-y^n$ into $(x-y)$ times n terms, each between $y^{n-1}$ and $x^{n-1}$.',[(r'Bound $b^3-a^3$ for $0<a<b$ using endpoint derivatives.',r'Integrating $3t^2$ from a to b gives $3a^2(b-a)\le b^3-a^3\le3b^2(b-a)$. Both inequalities are strict when a<b.'),(r'Bound $\log b-\log a$ for $0<a<b$.',r'The derivative $1/t$ is decreasing and lies between $1/b$ and $1/a$. Integration gives $(b-a)/b\le\log b-\log a\le(b-a)/a$, reversing which endpoint supplies the larger derivative.')])

add(66,'Real Analysis',r'For $f(x)=x$ on $[0,1]$, let $P_n$ divide the interval into n equal parts and $A_n=U(f,P_n)-L(f,P_n)$. Which statements are true?',[r'$\lim_{n\to\infty}nA_n=0$',r'$\sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n$ converges',r'$A_n$ is strictly monotonically decreasing',r'$\sum_{n=1}^\infty A_nA_{n+1}=1$'],'CD',[r'Each subinterval has width $1/n$ and function oscillation $1/n$.',r'After finding $A_n$, use a telescoping identity for $1/[n(n+1)]$.'],r'On every one of the n subintervals the supremum minus infimum is $1/n$ and its width is $1/n$. Thus $A_n=n/n^2=1/n$. Consequently $nA_n=1$, so A is false; $\sum A_n$ is the divergent harmonic series, so B is false. Since $1/(n+1)<1/n$, C holds. Finally $\sum A_nA_{n+1}=\sum1/[n(n+1)]=\sum(1/n-1/(n+1))=1$, proving D. The correct options are C and D.',r'Upper and lower Darboux sums multiply each subinterval length by the supremum and infimum of the function on that subinterval. Their difference is the sum of interval lengths times oscillations. For a monotone function, these extrema are its endpoint values; equal partitions often make the total oscillation telescope. Riemann integrability ensures that suitably refined upper and lower sums can approach each other, but gives no blanket convergence claim for the series of their differences. A sequence tending to zero may have a divergent sum, as $1/n$ does. Products of neighboring harmonic terms give $1/[n(n+1)]$, whose partial fractions form a telescoping series. Evaluate a telescoping series through its finite partial sums before taking the limit, and distinguish $A_n\to0$ from the faster rate asserted by $nA_n\to0$.',[(r'For $f(x)=x$ on $[0,2]$ with n equal intervals, compute the upper-minus-lower sum.',r'Each width and oscillation is $2/n$. The total difference is $n(2/n)^2=4/n$.'),(r'Evaluate $\sum_{n=1}^\infty1/[(n+1)(n+2)]$.',r'The summand is $1/(n+1)-1/(n+2)$. The partial sum through N is $1/2-1/(N+2)$, tending to $1/2$.')])

add(67,'Real Analysis',r'For nonnegative integers n,k, define $f_{n,k}(x)=x^n\sin(\pi/(2x))-x^k$ for $x\ne0$ in $[0,1]$, and $f_{n,k}(0)=0$. In which cases is it of bounded variation?',[r'For all $n\ge1$ and all $k\ge0$',r'For all $n\ge0$ and $k=0$',r'For all $n\ge1$ and all $k\ge2$',r'For all $n\ge2$ and all $k\ge0$'],'D',[r'Subtracting a bounded-variation power function cannot remove infinite variation.',r'The oscillatory derivative contains an $x^{n-2}$ term; the borderline n=1 needs an oscillation argument.'],r'For $h_n=x^n\sin(c/x)$ with $c=\pi/2$, $h_n\prime=nx^{n-1}\sin(c/x)-cx^{n-2}\cos(c/x)$. If $n\ge2$, this derivative is absolutely integrable near zero, and the continuous extension is of bounded variation. The subtracted $x^k$ also has finite variation; for k=0 the prescribed zero value at zero introduces only a finite jump. For n=1, choose extrema $x_j=1/(2j+1)$, where $\sin(c/x_j)=(-1)^j$. Variation along consecutive extrema is at least $\sum_j(x_j+x_{j+1})$, which diverges harmonically. For n=0 the same extrema have values $\pm1$, giving infinite variation. A and C include n=1, and B includes n=0, so each is false; D is true.',r'The total variation of f on an interval is the supremum, over all finite partitions, of the sum of absolute increments. A function has bounded variation if this supremum is finite. Sums and differences of bounded-variation functions remain of bounded variation, and a finite jump contributes finite variation. For an absolutely continuous function, total variation equals the integral of the absolute derivative, which gives a convenient sufficient criterion when a derivative is integrable. Oscillatory expressions approaching an endpoint can be tested through sequences of alternating extrema; their increment sums may diverge even when the function itself tends to zero. The oscillation amplitude must decay fast enough relative to its frequency. A continuous function can have infinite variation, and subtracting a smooth power cannot cancel that defect because otherwise the original function would be a sum of two bounded-variation functions.',[(r'Is $x^3\sin(1/x)$, extended by zero, of bounded variation on $[0,1]$?',r'Its derivative is $3x^2\sin(1/x)-x\cos(1/x)$, absolutely integrable. The extension is absolutely continuous and has finite variation.'),(r'Is $\sin(1/x)$, assigned any finite value at zero, of bounded variation?',r'No. Alternating extrema arbitrarily near zero take values 1 and $-1$. Each neighboring pair contributes 2, so partitions with arbitrarily many such points have arbitrarily large total variation.')])

add(68,'Multivariable Calculus',r'Let $f(x,y)=(u(x,y),v(x,y)):\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R^2$ be differentiable, with derivative matrix A at zero. Assume $f(y,-x)=(v(x,y),-u(x,y))$ everywhere. Which matrices A are possible?',[r'$\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\-1&-2\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}$'],'ABD',[r'Express the symmetry using the quarter-turn matrix J.',r'Differentiating $f(Jz)=Jf(z)$ at zero gives $AJ=JA$.'],r'Let $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$. The given identity is $f\circ J=J\circ f$, and differentiating at zero yields $AJ=JA$. For $A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ this requires $d=a$ and $c=-b$. Thus A must have form $\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-b&a\end{pmatrix}$. Options A, B and D meet this requirement; C does not. Sufficiency is demonstrated by the linear map $f(z)=Az$ for each admissible A, which satisfies the symmetry globally. Thus all and only A, B and D are possible.',r'Equivariance means that applying a symmetry to the input has the same effect as applying it to the output. For a differentiable map and a linear symmetry fixing the base point, the chain rule passes this relation to the derivative, so the derivative must commute with the symmetry matrix. In two dimensions, matrices commuting with a quarter-turn have equal diagonal entries and opposite off-diagonal entries. They correspond to multiplication by a complex scalar after identifying the plane with the complex numbers; the orientation convention determines the sign of the imaginary coordinate. A necessary derivative constraint becomes sufficient for a possible derivative by constructing a linear map with that matrix. Do not assume that every function with this discrete symmetry is holomorphic; the symmetry constrains its derivative at the fixed point but does not impose the Cauchy–Riemann equations at every other point.',[(r'Find all real $2\times2$ matrices commuting with $\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}$.',r'Comparing the two products forces both off-diagonal entries to zero. Thus all diagonal matrices commute with this reflection, and these are exactly the possibilities.'),(r'Does $A=\begin{pmatrix}3&4\\-4&3\end{pmatrix}$ commute with a quarter-turn?',r'Yes; it has equal diagonal entries and opposite off-diagonal entries. Direct multiplication gives $AJ=JA=\begin{pmatrix}-4&3\\-3&-4\end{pmatrix}$ for the stated J.')])

add(69,'Multivariable Calculus',r'Which listed functions admit a local inverse on an open neighborhood of $f(p)$?',[r'$p=(1,0)$, $f(x,y)=(x^3e^y+y-2x,\,2xy+2x)$',r'$p=(1,\pi)$, $f(r,\theta)=(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)$',r'$p=0$, $f(x)=x+2x^2\sin(1/x)$ for $x\ne0$, $f(0)=0$',r'$p=0$, $f(x)=x^2\sin(1/x)$ for $x\ne0$, $f(0)=0$'],'AB',[r'For the smooth planar maps evaluate the Jacobian determinant.',r'For C a nonzero derivative at the point alone is not enough if nearby derivatives oscillate and the map is not monotone.'],r'A has Jacobian at $(1,0)$ equal to $\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\2&2\end{pmatrix}$ with determinant $-2$, so the smooth inverse function theorem applies. B has determinant r, hence determinant 1 at $(1,\pi)$ and is locally invertible despite global angular periodicity. C has $f\prime(0)=1$, but for nonzero x its derivative is $1+4x\sin(1/x)-2\cos(1/x)$. At $x=1/(2\pi n)$ it equals $-1$; at $x=1/((2n+1)\pi)$ it equals 3. These points occur in every neighborhood of zero, so the continuous function is neither increasing nor decreasing there, hence not injective on any interval around zero. D has infinitely many zeros $x=1/(n\pi)$ accumulating at zero, so is not locally injective. Therefore A and B are correct.',r'The inverse function theorem states that a continuously differentiable map between equal-dimensional Euclidean spaces with an invertible derivative at a point has a continuously differentiable inverse on suitable open neighborhoods. Smoothness near the point, not just differentiability at the point, is part of the hypothesis. Global injectivity is unnecessary; polar coordinates near a positive-radius point give a standard local example despite angle periodicity. For a continuous real-valued function on an interval, injectivity is equivalent to strict monotonicity. A derivative taking both positive and negative values in every interval around a point therefore obstructs local injectivity. A zero derivative alone does not prove failure of inversion, since $x^3$ is invertible; explicit repeated values or loss of monotonicity are stronger arguments. Always verify the exact theorem hypotheses before using a nonzero derivative as a certificate.',[(r'Does $f(x)=x^3$ have a local inverse at zero?',r'Yes: it is strictly increasing everywhere and its inverse is the real cube root. Its derivative is zero at zero, so the smooth inverse theorem does not apply and the inverse is not differentiable there.'),(r'Is $(r,\theta)\mapsto(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)$ locally invertible at r=0?',r'No. All points $(0,\theta)$ map to $(0,0)$, including distinct nearby angles. The Jacobian determinant r vanishes, and this explicit collision establishes failure of local injectivity.')])

add(70,'Functional Analysis',r'Let $C_0(\mathbb R)$ be continuous functions with $f(x)\to0$ as $|x|\to\infty$, equipped with the uniform norm. For $f_n,f\in C_0(\mathbb R)$, which statements are correct?',[r'Uniform convergence on compact sets implies $\|f_n-f\|\to0$',r'$\|f_n-f\|\to0$ implies uniform convergence on compact sets',r'Uniform convergence on compact sets is equivalent to $\|f_n-f\|\to0$',r'Neither convergence implies the other'],'B',[r'The supremum on a compact subset is bounded by the supremum over the whole line.',r'Consider a fixed-height compactly supported bump translated toward infinity.'],r'For any compact K, $\sup_{x\in K}|f_n(x)-f(x)|\le\|f_n-f\|_\infty$, so B is true. Conversely let $\phi(x)=\max(1-|x|,0)$ and $f_n(x)=\phi(x-n)$, with $f=0$. Each is in $C_0(\mathbb R)$; on every fixed compact set, $f_n$ is eventually identically zero. Yet $\|f_n\|_\infty=1$ for every n. Thus A and C fail; D fails because the implication in B always holds. Only B is correct.',r'The uniform norm measures the supremum over the entire domain, and its convergence controls every subset simultaneously. Compact-uniform convergence measures the supremum on each fixed compact subset, allowing the relevant index to depend on that subset. On a noncompact domain, mass or peaks can escape to infinity, making compact convergence strictly weaker than global uniform convergence. Membership of each function in $C_0$ means that each individual function vanishes at infinity, but does not give a uniform tail bound over an entire sequence. A translated compactly supported bump is therefore a useful counterexample. If a sequence additionally has uniformly small tails, compact-uniform convergence can imply norm convergence by splitting the domain into one large compact set and its complement. The distinction lies in the order and uniformity of the quantifiers.',[(r'For $f_n(x)=e^{-x^2}/n$, compare the two convergence notions.',r'The supremum is $1/n$, so $f_n\to0$ in uniform norm. The same bound controls every compact subset, establishing compact-uniform convergence as well.'),(r'For $f_n(x)=e^{-(x-n)^2}$, does compact-uniform convergence imply norm convergence here?',r'On a fixed compact set, the distance to n tends uniformly to infinity, so $f_n\to0$ there. Its supremum is always 1 at x=n, so there is no convergence in uniform norm.')])

add(71,'Linear Algebra',r'A is an $m\times n$ matrix. $A(1,:)$ deletes row 1, $A(:,1)$ deletes column 1, and $A(1,1)$ deletes both. Which statements hold?',[r'$\operatorname{rank}A-2\le\operatorname{rank}A(1,1)\le\operatorname{rank}A$',r'$\operatorname{rank}A(1,:)=\operatorname{rank}A(:,1)$',r'If $\operatorname{rank}A=\operatorname{rank}A(1,:)=\operatorname{rank}A(:,1)$, then $\operatorname{rank}A=\operatorname{rank}A(1,1)$',r'$\operatorname{rank}A(1,:)+\operatorname{rank}A(:,1)+2\ge2\operatorname{rank}A$'],'ACD',[r'Deleting one row or one column reduces rank by at most one.',r'For C, use that row 1 is a linear combination of the remaining rows and restrict that relation after column deletion.'],r'A holds by deleting one row and one column successively, each reducing rank by at most one, and submatrices cannot increase rank. B is false for $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$: deleting row 1 leaves rank 1 while deleting column 1 leaves rank 0. For C, equality with the row-deleted rank means row 1 is a combination of the other rows. The same relation survives deleting column 1, so deleting row 1 from $A(:,1)$ does not reduce rank. Its rank already equals rank A by hypothesis, proving C. D follows by adding the inequalities $\operatorname{rank}A(1,:)\ge\operatorname{rank}A-1$ and $\operatorname{rank}A(:,1)\ge\operatorname{rank}A-1$. Thus A,C,D hold.',r'Matrix rank is both the dimension of the row space and the dimension of the column space. Removing one row can reduce the row-span dimension by at most one; the corresponding statement applies to removing one column. Every submatrix has rank at most that of the original matrix. Equality after row deletion means that the deleted row was dependent on the retained rows. Any coordinate restriction preserves a linear dependence, which is why that row remains redundant after deleting a column. This elementary dependency argument proves stronger statements than merely combining the numerical rank bounds. Row deletion and column deletion need not have identical effects: the structure of a particular row and column matters. When checking a universal rank identity, tiny matrices with a zero column or repeated rows often give decisive counterexamples.',[(r'Can deleting one row reduce rank by two?',r'No. Restoring one row adds one vector to the row span and raises its dimension by at most one. Therefore the original rank exceeds the row-deleted rank by at most one.'),(r'For $A=I_3$, delete its first row and first column. What ranks result?',r'A has rank 3. Each single deletion has rank 2. Deleting both yields $I_2$ of rank 2, showing that two deletions need not cause a total rank loss of two.')])

add(72,'Linear Algebra',r'Let $M\in M_n(\mathbb R)$ with $M\ne0,I_n$ and $M^2=M$. Which statements are true?',[r'$\operatorname{Null}(M)$ is the eigenspace for eigenvalue 0',r'Every nonzero $X\in\operatorname{Col}(M)$ is an eigenvector for eigenvalue 1',r'Every $X\notin\operatorname{Null}(M)$ is an eigenvector for eigenvalue 1',r'$\mathbb R^n=\operatorname{Col}(M)+\operatorname{Null}(M)$'],'ABD',[r'An idempotent map fixes its image and annihilates its kernel.',r'Decompose any vector as $X=MX+(X-MX)$.'],r'A is true by the definition $MX=0X$. Since $M\ne I$ and is idempotent, the kernel is nonzero: otherwise M would be invertible and $M^2=M$ would imply M=I. For B, write $X=MY$; then $MX=M^2Y=MY=X$, so every nonzero image vector is a 1-eigenvector. C fails for $M=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$ and $X=(1,1)^T$: it is not in the kernel but $MX=(1,0)^T\ne X$. D holds because $MX$ lies in the image and $M(X-MX)=0$. The sum is in fact direct, since an image vector in the kernel is both fixed and annihilated. Thus A,B,D are correct.',r'An idempotent linear map satisfies $M^2=M$ and is a projection onto its image along its kernel. It need not be an orthogonal projection unless it is also symmetric in the real standard inner product. Idempotence forces every eigenvalue to satisfy $\lambda^2=\lambda$, hence to be 0 or 1. The polynomial $t(t-1)$ has distinct roots, so every idempotent matrix is diagonalizable. Every vector decomposes uniquely into its image component $MX$ and kernel component $(I-M)X$. Being outside the kernel only says that the image component is nonzero; it does not remove the kernel component or make the vector itself fixed. Nonzero and nonidentity assumptions ensure that both eigenspaces occur. The rank is the dimension of the 1-eigenspace and the nullity is the dimension of the 0-eigenspace.',[(r'For $M=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}$, find image and kernel.',r'$M(x,y)=(x+y,0)$, so the image is span$(1,0)$ and kernel is span$(1,-1)$. The map is idempotent but not symmetric, giving an oblique projection.'),(r'Decompose $(3,4)$ for $M=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$.',r'The image component is $M(3,4)=(3,0)$ and the kernel component is $(0,4)$. Their sum is the original vector, and their respective subspaces intersect only at zero.')])

add(73,'Linear Algebra',r'Let $W_1=\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-b&2a\end{pmatrix}:a,b\in\mathbb R\}$ and $W_2=\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\-b&-a\end{pmatrix}:a,b\in\mathbb R\}$ in $M_2(\mathbb R)$. Which statements are true?',[r'$W_1\cap W_2=\{0\}$',r'$\{0\}$ is a proper subset of $W_1\cap W_2$',r'$W_1+W_2=M_2(\mathbb R)$',r'$W_1+W_2$ is a proper subset of $M_2(\mathbb R)$'],'BD',[r'Compare the two diagonal entries for an element in the intersection.',r'Both spaces have dimension 2 and share the skew-symmetric off-diagonal direction.'],r'An intersection element has matching top-left parameters a and lower-right entries $2a=-a$, forcing $a=0$. Its b parameter is arbitrary, so $W_1\cap W_2=\{\begin{pmatrix}0&b\\-b&0\end{pmatrix}\}$ has dimension 1. Thus A is false and B is true. The dimension formula gives $\dim(W_1+W_2)=2+2-1=3<4=\dim M_2(\mathbb R)$, so C is false and D is true. Explicitly the sum consists of every matrix $\begin{pmatrix}s&t\\-t&u\end{pmatrix}$ with independent s,t,u; it excludes matrices whose off-diagonal entries are not opposites.',r'The sum of subspaces U and V is the set of all vectors u+v with u in U and v in V, and it is itself a subspace. Their intersection consists of vectors satisfying both defining constraints. The dimension formula $\dim(U+V)=\dim U+\dim V-\dim(U\cap V)$ corrects for directions counted twice. Parameter descriptions must be compared using independent parameters before equating entries; do not assume that identically named parameters in two different definitions are automatically the same until membership equality forces it. A nonzero shared direction disproves a trivial-intersection claim. A dimension strictly below the ambient dimension proves that a subspace is proper. In spaces of matrices, comparing entries and identifying common structural constraints often makes both intersection and sum particularly transparent.',[(r'In $\mathbb R^3$, let U=span$(e_1,e_2)$ and V=span$(e_2,e_3)$. Find intersection and sum.',r'The intersection is span$(e_2)$, dimension 1. The sum contains all three standard basis vectors, hence is $\mathbb R^3$; its dimension is $2+2-1=3$.'),(r'Can two two-dimensional subspaces of $\mathbb R^4$ have a three-dimensional sum?',r'Yes, exactly when their intersection has dimension 1. For example U=span$(e_1,e_2)$ and V=span$(e_2,e_3)$ have the required sum dimension.')])

add(74,'Linear Algebra',r'Let A be a $3\times3$ nilpotent matrix. Which statements are necessarily true?',[r'$(I+A)^n=I$ for some integer $n>0$',r'The column space of A is $\{0\}$',r'The eigenvalues of A are roots of 1',r'$A^3$ is diagonalizable'],'D',[r'A nilpotent matrix in dimension three has only eigenvalue zero.',r'Cayley–Hamilton gives $A^3=0$.'],r'Nilpotence implies every eigenvalue is zero, so the characteristic polynomial is $t^3$. Cayley–Hamilton gives $A^3=0$, and the zero matrix is diagonal, proving D. C is false because zero is not a root of unity. B is false for the nonzero nilpotent $A=E_{12}$, whose column space is span$(e_1)$. For that same example $A^2=0$ and $(I+A)^n=I+nA\ne I$ for every positive integer n over $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, disproving A. Thus only D is necessary.',r'A nilpotent matrix has a positive power equal to zero. Applying that power to any eigenvector shows that its eigenvalue must be zero. For an n-dimensional matrix, this gives characteristic polynomial $t^n$, and Cayley–Hamilton bounds the nilpotency index by n. A nilpotent matrix is diagonalizable only if it is zero, since a diagonalizable matrix with all eigenvalues zero is similar to the zero diagonal matrix. Nevertheless a sufficiently high power is zero and therefore diagonalizable. Adding the identity produces a unipotent matrix with eigenvalues one, but eigenvalues being roots of unity does not itself imply the matrix has finite order; nontrivial Jordan blocks prevent that conclusion in characteristic zero. Nilpotence is also distinct from a zero column space, which would force the original matrix itself to vanish.',[(r'If a $5\times5$ matrix is nilpotent, what power is guaranteed to vanish?',r'All eigenvalues are zero, so its characteristic polynomial is $t^5$. Cayley–Hamilton gives $A^5=0$, though a smaller power may already be zero.'),(r'Can a nonzero real nilpotent matrix be symmetric?',r'No. Real symmetric matrices are orthogonally diagonalizable. Nilpotence forces all diagonalized eigenvalues to zero, hence the matrix itself is zero.')])

add(75,'Linear Algebra',r'A is $100\times100$ with $a_{ij}=i$ when $i+j=101$ and zero otherwise. Which statements are true?',[r'A is similar to a diagonal matrix over $\mathbb R$',r'A is not similar to a diagonal matrix over $\mathbb C$',r'One eigenvalue of A is 10',r'None of the real eigenvalues exceeds 51'],'ACD',[r'Pair coordinates i and $101-i$.',r'Each pair gives a $2\times2$ block with eigenvalues $\pm\sqrt{i(101-i)}$.'],r'For $i=1,\ldots,50$, span$(e_i,e_{101-i})$ is invariant, and its matrix is $\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\101-i&0\end{pmatrix}$. Its eigenvalues are the distinct real values $\pm\sqrt{i(101-i)}$, so every block is diagonalizable over the reals, proving A and disproving B. At i=1, the pair is $\pm\sqrt{100}=\pm10$, giving C. Since $i(101-i)\le(101/2)^2$, every positive eigenvalue is at most $50.5<51$, and negative eigenvalues also do not exceed 51. Thus D holds. More exactly the largest eigenvalue is $\sqrt{50\cdot51}=\sqrt{2550}$.',r'An antidiagonal matrix maps a standard basis vector into the coordinate paired with it across the antidiagonal. In even dimension this decomposes the vector space into independent two-dimensional invariant subspaces. A block $\begin{pmatrix}0&a\\b&0\end{pmatrix}$ has characteristic polynomial $\lambda^2-ab$. If ab is positive, its eigenvalues are distinct and real, so it is diagonalizable over the reals even if the block is not symmetric. A direct sum of diagonalizable blocks is diagonalizable. To bound the eigenvalues, bound the products ab; the quadratic $i(N-i)$ is maximized near N/2. Real diagonalizability automatically implies complex diagonalizability, so claims denying the latter cannot coexist with the former. Distinguish an entry value from an eigenvalue; block structure, rather than the diagonal entries, determines the spectrum here.',[(r'Find the eigenvalues of $\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\8&0\end{pmatrix}$.',r'The characteristic polynomial is $\lambda^2-16$, so eigenvalues are 4 and $-4$. They are distinct real numbers, proving real diagonalizability.'),(r'For a $4\times4$ antidiagonal matrix with row weights 1,2,3,4, find its spectrum.',r'Pair rows 1 and 4 to get eigenvalues $\pm\sqrt{1\cdot4}=\pm2$; pair rows 2 and 3 to get $\pm\sqrt6$. The union is the full spectrum.')])

add(76,'Linear Algebra',r'Let $v_1,v_2,v_3$ be an orthonormal basis of $\mathbb R^3$, and let V have these vectors as columns. Which statements are necessarily true?',[r'$VV^T=I$',r'$V^TV=I$',r'$V=V^T$',r'$\det V\ne0$'],'ABD',[r'Entries of $V^TV$ are inner products of columns.',r'For a square matrix, $V^TV=I$ implies $V^{-1}=V^T$.'],r'Orthonormality gives $(V^TV)_{ij}=v_i\cdot v_j=\delta_{ij}$, proving B. This makes V invertible with inverse $V^T$, and therefore $VV^T=I$, proving A. Determinants give $(\det V)^2=1$, so D is true. Symmetry C is not necessary: take a quarter-turn in the first two coordinates with V=$\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}$, whose columns are orthonormal but $V\ne V^T$. Thus A,B,D are correct.',r'An orthogonal real square matrix is characterized by $V^TV=I$, which says exactly that its columns form an orthonormal basis. Its transpose is its inverse, so its rows are also orthonormal and $VV^T=I$. Orthogonal transformations preserve inner products, lengths and angles, and their determinant is either 1 or $-1$, distinguishing orientation-preserving and orientation-reversing transformations. Orthogonality is a relation involving a product with the transpose; it is not the same as symmetry, which equates the matrix itself with its transpose. A rotation is typically orthogonal without being symmetric. For rectangular matrices, orthonormal columns give $V^TV=I$ but $VV^T$ is generally a projection rather than the identity, so the square-matrix hypothesis matters. A set of n orthonormal vectors in n dimensions automatically forms a basis.',[(r'Is a planar rotation by $\pi/3$ symmetric?',r'Its matrix is $\begin{pmatrix}1/2&-\sqrt3/2\\\sqrt3/2&1/2\end{pmatrix}$. Its columns are orthonormal, but its off-diagonal entries differ, so it is orthogonal and not symmetric.'),(r'If a real matrix is both orthogonal and symmetric, what does its square equal?',r'Orthogonality gives $V^TV=I$ and symmetry gives $V^T=V$, hence $V^2=I$. Its eigenvalues can therefore only be 1 or $-1$.')])

add(77,'Topology',r'For a function $f:[0,1]\to(0,1)$, which statements are FALSE?',[r'If f is onto, then f is continuous',r'If f is continuous, then f is not onto',r'If f is one-to-one, then f is continuous',r'If f is continuous, then f is not one-to-one'],'ACD',[r'Use compactness to rule out continuous surjections onto an open interval.',r'An affine map into the interval can be injective; swapping two of its output values preserves injectivity but destroys continuity.'],r'B is true: the continuous image of compact [0,1] is compact, whereas (0,1) is not compact, so no continuous map is onto. A is false: define $f(x)=x$ for $0<x<1$ and $f(0)=f(1)=1/2$; it is onto but discontinuous. D is false for the continuous injection $f(x)=(x+1)/3$. C is false as well: start with this affine injection and swap its values at x=1/3 and x=2/3, leaving all other values unchanged. The resulting map is still injective and lies in (0,1), but is discontinuous at the two swapped points. Therefore the requested false statements are A,C,D.',r'Continuity preserves compactness, so a compact domain cannot map continuously onto a noncompact target. This obstruction depends on the topology of the entire target, not just on cardinality or whether it fits inside the real line. Injectivity and surjectivity are set-theoretic properties and alone do not imply continuity; discontinuous rearrangements can preserve either property. A continuous injection from a compact space into a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism onto its image, but it need not cover the given target. For interval-valued functions, affine maps furnish easy continuous injections with compact image strictly inside an open target interval. When a question asks which implications are false, distinguish a false implication from a true conclusion occurring for some examples: a single counterexample must satisfy the premise and violate the conclusion.',[(r'Can [0,1] map continuously onto $\mathbb R$?',r'No. Its continuous image is compact and therefore bounded in $\mathbb R$, while the whole real line is unbounded and noncompact.'),(r'Give a continuous injection from [0,1] into (2,3).',r'$f(x)=9/4+x/2$ has image $[9/4,11/4]\subset(2,3)$. Its positive slope makes it injective, and being affine makes it continuous.')])

add(78,'Linear Algebra',r'Let $y_1,y_2,y_3,y_4$ be an orthonormal basis of $\mathbb R^4$. Which lists are orthonormal bases?',[r'$\{y_1+y_2,y_1-y_2,y_3,y_4\}$',r'$\{y_3,y_4,y_1,y_2\}$',r'$\{(y_1+y_2)/2,(y_1-y_2)/2,y_3,y_4\}$',r'$\{(3y_1+4y_2)/5,(4y_1-3y_2)/5,y_3,y_4\}$'],'BD',[r'Check both pairwise orthogonality and unit lengths.',r'The first pair in D has squared lengths $(9+16)/25$ and inner product $(12-12)/25$.'],r'B is a permutation of an orthonormal basis, so it is an orthonormal basis. In A the first two vectors are orthogonal but each has norm $\sqrt2$, not 1. In C their norms are $\sqrt2/2$, also not 1. D has first two squared norms 1 and mutual inner product zero; each is orthogonal to $y_3,y_4$ because it lies in span$(y_1,y_2)$. Four orthonormal vectors in $\mathbb R^4$ form a basis. Thus B and D are correct.',r'Orthonormality combines two requirements: distinct vectors have zero inner product and every vector has unit norm. An orthogonal nonzero set is linearly independent, and a set of n such vectors in n-dimensional space forms a basis. Coordinates with respect to an orthonormal basis permit direct norm and inner-product calculation by ordinary Euclidean formulas. Replacing a pair by a linear combination matrix preserves orthonormality exactly when that coefficient matrix is orthogonal. The common sum-and-difference transformation must use a factor $1/\sqrt2$, rather than $1/2$, to normalize its vectors. Pythagorean triples such as 3,4,5 similarly provide normalized combinations. Permuting vectors preserves all inner products and hence preserves orthonormality. Merely spanning the same subspace is insufficient when the question specifically requires an orthonormal basis.',[(r'Normalize $y_1+y_2$ and $y_1-y_2$.',r'Each has norm $\sqrt2$ and their inner product is zero. Dividing both by $\sqrt2$ gives an orthonormal pair.'),(r'Are $(5y_1+12y_2)/13$ and $(12y_1-5y_2)/13$ orthonormal?',r'Each squared norm is $(25+144)/169=1$, and the mutual inner product is $(60-60)/169=0$. Yes, they form an orthonormal pair spanning the same two-dimensional subspace.')])

add(79,'Number Theory',r'Let $S=\{e^{2\pi i\theta}:\theta\in\mathbb Q\}$. For each $z\in S$, what properties has $\{z^{n!}:n\text{ is a positive integer}\}$?',[r'Countable',r'Countably infinite',r'Uncountable',r'Finite'],'AD',[r'A rational-angle point is a root of unity.',r'If its order is q, then q divides n! for all $n\ge q$.'],r'Write $z=e^{2\pi ip/q}$ for integers p and positive q. Then $z^q=1$. For every $n\ge q$, the factorial n! is divisible by q and $z^{n!}=1$. Only the finitely many earlier values can differ, so the set is finite, D. Every finite set is countable in the standard “at most countable” sense, so A also holds. B and C are false. Even without rationality, an image of the positive integers is at most countable, but rationality supplies eventual constancy here.',r'A complex root of unity has finite multiplicative order q, meaning its qth power is one. Rational arguments modulo one give precisely the roots of unity. For factorial exponents, any fixed positive integer q divides n! once n is at least q, so roots of unity yield eventually constant factorial-power sequences. The range of any integer-indexed sequence is at most countable regardless of its values, but may be finite, countably infinite, or a singleton. In the usual set-theoretic terminology, countable includes finite sets; “countably infinite” explicitly excludes them. When two options use these related terms, a multiple-correct question can legitimately accept both “countable” and “finite.” Distinguish the sequence of infinitely many terms from its range, which may contain only finitely many distinct values.',[(r'List the range of $i^{n!}$ for $n\ge1$.',r'At n=1 it is i, at n=2 it is $-1$, at n=3 it is $-1$, and for n≥4 the exponent is divisible by 4, giving 1. The range is $\{i,-1,1\}$.'),(r'If $z^5=1$, when is $z^{n!}$ guaranteed to equal one?',r'For every n≥5, the factor 5 occurs in n!, so the exponent is a multiple of 5. Thus all those terms equal one, irrespective of which fifth root z is.')])

add(80,'Complex Analysis',r'For $c>0$, consider $\left|(z-i)/(z+i)\right|=c$. Which statements are true?',[r'If $c>1$, the locus is a circle centered on the imaginary axis',r'If $c<1$, the locus is a circle centered on the real axis',r'If $c=1$, the locus is a straight line parallel to the imaginary axis',r'If $c=1$, the locus is a straight line not passing through the origin'],'A',[r'Square the ratio and write $z=x+iy$.',r'For $c=1$ the equation reduces to y=0.'],r'The equation is $x^2+(y-1)^2=c^2[x^2+(y+1)^2]$. For $c\ne1$, completion of squares gives $x^2+(y-(1+c^2)/(1-c^2))^2=4c^2/(1-c^2)^2$. Its center lies on the imaginary axis and is not on the real axis, so A is true and B false. For $c=1$ it reduces to $-2y=2y$, hence y=0: the real axis, through the origin. Thus C and D are false. The excluded denominator point $z=-i$ does not satisfy the squared locus anyway, so it creates no missing locus point.',r'An Apollonius locus specifies a fixed ratio of distances from two distinct points. If the ratio is not one, the locus is a circle; if it is one, the locus is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the points. For points i and $-i$, that segment is vertical, so its perpendicular bisector is the horizontal real axis. Squaring the modulus is valid because both sides are nonnegative, but the original denominator must remain nonzero. Completing the Cartesian quadratic identifies both center and radius and avoids guessing orientation from a diagram. The circle centers lie on the line joining the foci. Positive ratios below and above one lie on opposite sides, but both have centers on the same axis. As the ratio approaches one, the circle radius tends to infinity and the limiting locus becomes a line.',[(r'Find $|z-1|=|z+1|$.',r'Writing z=x+iy gives $(x-1)^2+y^2=(x+1)^2+y^2$, so x=0. The locus is the imaginary axis, the perpendicular bisector of the two real foci.'),(r'Find the center and radius for $|(z-i)/(z+i)|=2$.',r'The center coordinate is $(1+4)/(1-4)=-5/3$, and radius is $2\cdot2/|1-4|=4/3$. Thus the circle has center $-5i/3$ and radius $4/3$.')])

add(81,'Complex Analysis',r'For z in $\mathbb C$, $\Re z$ is its real part. An entire function satisfies $|f(z)|\le|z||\Re z|$ everywhere. Which statements are true?',[r'$f(0)=0$',r'$f\prime(0)=0$',r'The only such entire function is $f\equiv0$',r'There exists a nonconstant entire function with this property'],'ABC',[r'What does the bound say on the imaginary axis?',r'An entire function vanishing on a set with a finite accumulation point is identically zero.'],r'At every $z=it$, the bound is $|f(it)|\le|it|\cdot0=0$, so f vanishes on the imaginary axis. These zeros accumulate at zero, which belongs to the domain, and the identity theorem gives $f\equiv0$. Consequently A, B and C are true, while D is false. Independently, A follows directly at zero and B follows from $|f(z)/z|\le|\Re z|\to0$ for $z\to0$, but the identity theorem gives the strongest conclusion.',r'The identity theorem states that a holomorphic function on a connected open domain with zeros accumulating at an interior point must vanish identically. Its proof relies on the first nonzero Taylor coefficient: a nonzero holomorphic function has isolated zeros near any point. Entire functions have the connected domain $\mathbb C$, so vanishing on a line, curve, or sequence with a finite accumulation point is decisive. Inequality hypotheses can force exact zeros where their right-hand side vanishes; inspect those sets before using global growth estimates. To infer a derivative at zero from a bound, first establish f(0)=0 and then estimate the difference quotient. An accumulation only at infinity would not suffice, as a nonzero entire function such as sine has infinitely many zeros without being identically zero.',[(r'If an entire f satisfies $|f(z)|\le|\Im z|$, determine f.',r'It vanishes at every real z. The real line has interior-domain accumulation points, so the identity theorem implies $f\equiv0$.'),(r'Does an entire function with zeros at every integer have to vanish identically?',r'No. $f(z)=\sin(\pi z)$ is a nonzero entire function with precisely such zeros. The integer zero set has no finite accumulation point in $\mathbb C$.')])

add(82,'Complex Analysis',r'Let $\mathbb D$ be the open unit disk. For holomorphic $f:\mathbb D\to\mathbb D$ with $f(0)=1/2$, which listed values of $|f\prime(0)|$ are possible?',[r'$7/8$',r'$5/6$',r'$3/4$',r'$1$'],'C',[r'Use Schwarz–Pick at zero.',r'The derivative bound is $1-|f(0)|^2$, and a disk automorphism attains it.'],r'Schwarz–Pick gives $|f\prime(0)|\le1-|f(0)|^2=3/4$. Thus A, B and D exceed the bound. C is attained by the disk automorphism $f(z)=(z+1/2)/(1+z/2)$, which maps the disk onto itself, has value $1/2$ at zero, and derivative $1-(1/2)^2=3/4$ there. Hence exactly C is possible.',r'Schwarz lemma bounds $|g\prime(0)|$ by one for a holomorphic disk self-map satisfying g(0)=0, with equality only for rotations. Schwarz–Pick follows by composing a general disk self-map with automorphisms that move the base point and its image to zero. Its derivative inequality is $|f\prime(z)|/(1-|f(z)|^2)\le1/(1-|z|^2)$. At zero, prescribing f(0)=a leaves the bound $1-|a|^2$. Automorphisms attain equality, so the bound is sharp even though the target is the open disk. Smaller magnitudes can be realized by composing a suitable automorphism with a contraction before it. The Cauchy estimate gives a weaker bound here and cannot identify the sharp listed value. Always show attainability as well as exclusion when the question asks which values are possible.',[(r'For a disk self-map with f(0)=0, can $|f\prime(0)|=1$?',r'Yes: $f(z)=e^{i\theta}z$ attains 1 and maps the disk to itself. Schwarz lemma says these rotations are exactly the equality cases.'),(r'For f(0)=1/3, what is the largest derivative magnitude?',r'Schwarz–Pick gives $1-1/9=8/9$. The automorphism $(z+1/3)/(1+z/3)$ has that derivative at zero, so the maximum is attained.')])

add(83,'Number Theory',r'For positive integers n, let $A_n=\{(x,y,z)\in\mathbb N^3:x^n+y^n=z^n,\ z<n\}$ and $F(n)=|A_n|$. Which statements are true?',[r'$F(n)$ is always finite for $n\ge3$',r'$F(2)=\infty$',r'$F(n)=0$ for all n',r'$F(n)$ is nonzero for some $n>2$'],'AC',[r'The extra restriction $z<n$ makes all candidate sets finite.',r'Use Fermat’s last theorem for n>2 and inspect n=1,2 directly.'],r'For any solution with positive x,y, each is strictly less than z; with $z<n$, there are only finitely many candidate triples, proving A even without deep number theory. Fermat’s last theorem says no positive integers satisfy $x^n+y^n=z^n$ for n>2, so F(n)=0 there. For n=1, the bound $z<1$ permits no positive z. For n=2, only z=1 is allowed, but $x^2+y^2\ge2>1$. Therefore F(n)=0 for every positive n, proving C. B and D are false. The familiar infinitely many Pythagorean triples do not survive the imposed bound z<2.',r'Cardinality questions about Diophantine equations require attention to every auxiliary bound, not just the main equation. If positive powers sum to a third positive power, each summand base is smaller than the third base. A fixed upper bound on that third base therefore produces only finitely many possible triples. Fermat’s last theorem states that $x^n+y^n=z^n$ has no positive integer solution when the integer exponent exceeds two. For exponent two, infinitely many Pythagorean triples exist without bounds, but a sufficiently small imposed bound can eliminate all of them. For exponent one, ordinary additive triples exist without the bound, while z<1 again removes them. “Always finite” includes the case of an empty set. Prove small exceptional exponents directly rather than applying a theorem outside its stated range.',[(r'How many positive triples satisfy $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with z<5?',r'For z=1,2,3,4, no positive square pair sums to $z^2$. The first positive Pythagorean hypotenuse is 5, so the requested set is empty.'),(r'List ordered positive triples with $x+y=z$ and z<4.',r'For z=2 the pair is (1,1); for z=3 the pairs are (1,2) and (2,1); z=1 has none. Thus the triples are (1,1,2), (1,2,3), (2,1,3), totaling three.')])

add(84,'Number Theory',r'For Euler’s totient $\varphi(n)$ and n>3, which statements are true?',[r'$\varphi(n)$ can never divide n',r'$\varphi(n)\mid n$ implies there are integers x,y with $nx+6y=1$',r'$\varphi(n)\mid n$ implies n has at most two distinct prime divisors',r'$\varphi(n)\mid\varphi(nk)$ for every positive integer k'],'CD',[r'Use $\varphi(n)=n\prod_{p\mid n}(1-1/p)$.',r'For C compare powers of 2 in $\prod p$ and $\prod(p-1)$; for D separate new primes introduced by k.'],r'A fails at n=4, since $\varphi(4)=2\mid4$. B also fails at n=4: $4x+6y$ is always even. For C, if $\varphi(n)\mid n$, then $n/\varphi(n)=\prod_{p\mid n}p/\prod_{p\mid n}(p-1)$ is an integer. The numerator has at most one factor 2, while every odd prime divisor contributes at least one factor 2 to the denominator. Hence there can be at most one odd prime divisor and therefore at most two distinct prime divisors overall, proving C. For D, the totient formula yields $\varphi(nk)/\varphi(n)=k\prod_{p\mid k,\ p\nmid n}(p-1)/p$. Each new prime divides k, so their product divides k; the expression is an integer. Thus D holds universally.',r'Euler’s totient counts residues from 1 to n coprime to n and satisfies the prime-factor formula $\varphi(n)=n\prod_{p\mid n}(1-1/p)$. Divisibility questions can be converted to integrality of a ratio and then examined with prime valuations. The 2-adic valuation is particularly effective because every odd prime p makes p-1 even, whereas a product of distinct primes contains at most one factor 2. Bézout’s identity says that an integer equation $nx+6y=1$ is solvable exactly when $\gcd(n,6)=1$, making small even counterexamples decisive. Multiplying n by k introduces additional prime powers and perhaps new prime divisors; separating those new primes shows that the ratio of totients is integral. Keep distinct prime divisors separate from their exponents: the exponent count does not enter the product of p/(p-1).',[(r'Does $\varphi(12)$ divide 12?',r'$12=2^2\cdot3$, so $\varphi(12)=12(1/2)(2/3)=4$, which divides 12. The integer has exactly two distinct prime divisors.'),(r'Compute $\varphi(15\cdot2)/\varphi(15)$.',r'$\varphi(15)=8$ and $\varphi(30)=8$, so the ratio is 1. The formula also gives $2(1-1/2)=1$ because 2 is the only new prime.')])

add(85,'Number Theory',r'Let m>n be coprime positive integers. Which statements are true?',[r'$\gcd(m-n,m+n)=1$',r'$\gcd(m-n,m+n)$ can have a prime divisor',r'There exist integers x,y with $nx-my=3$',r'$\gcd(m-n,m+n)$ can be an odd prime'],'BC',[r'A common divisor divides both 2m and 2n.',r'Use coprimality to bound that gcd and Bézout to obtain the integer equation.'],r'If $d=\gcd(m-n,m+n)$, then d divides both 2m and 2n. Since $\gcd(m,n)=1$, d divides 2, so d is 1 or 2. At m=3,n=1, d=2, disproving A and proving the possibility in B. D is false because no odd prime divides 2. Bézout gives integers u,v with $nu+mv=1$. Multiply by 3 and take $x=3u,y=-3v$ to obtain $nx-my=3$, proving C. Thus B and C are correct.',r'The Euclidean algorithm preserves gcd under replacing one input by its difference with a multiple of the other. For sums and differences, a common divisor often divides twice the original entries; coprimality then restricts it to a small set of possibilities. Parity decides whether the residual divisor 2 actually occurs: coprime integers cannot both be even, and if they are both odd their sum and difference are even. Bézout’s identity says that an integer linear combination of two numbers equals their gcd, and every multiple of that gcd is likewise a linear combination. A linear Diophantine equation $ax+by=c$ is solvable exactly when $\gcd(a,b)$ divides c. The minus sign on one coefficient can be absorbed by changing the sign of the corresponding integer variable. Possibility claims need an example, whereas universal claims need a proof.',[(r'For coprime m=8,n=3, compute $\gcd(m-n,m+n)$.',r'The inputs are 5 and 11, whose gcd is 1. Different parity prevents a common factor 2.'),(r'Find integers x,y with $3x-5y=3$.',r'Taking x=1,y=0 gives 3 directly. More generally x=$1+5t$, y=$3t$ for integer t yields $3+15t-15t=3$.')])

add(86,'Abstract Algebra',r'Let G be cyclic of order 8 and $H=S_5$. Which statements are necessarily true?',[r'There is no nontrivial homomorphism $G\to H$',r'There is no injective homomorphism $G\to H$',r'There is no surjective homomorphism $G\to H$',r'There are more than 20 homomorphisms $G\to H$'],'BCD',[r'A homomorphism from a cyclic group is determined by the image of a generator.',r'The image permutation must have order dividing 8; inspect cycle types in $S_5$.'],r'A generator may map to any permutation h satisfying $h^8=e$, so allowed orders are 1,2,4; order 8 does not occur in $S_5$ because cycle lengths sum to 5 and their least common multiple cannot be 8. Thus no homomorphism is injective, proving B. Surjectivity is impossible from 8 elements onto 120, proving C. Mapping to a transposition gives a nontrivial homomorphism, so A is false. There are 1 identity, 10 transpositions, 15 products of two disjoint transpositions, and 30 four-cycles, all satisfying $h^8=e$. Hence there are 56 homomorphisms, proving D.',r'A homomorphism from a cyclic group of order n is specified uniquely by the image h of a generator, subject to $h^n=e$. Its kernel is trivial precisely when h has order n, and its image is the cyclic subgroup generated by h. The order of a permutation is the least common multiple of the lengths of its disjoint cycles. Counting candidate generator images therefore reduces to allowable cycle types. Transpositions, double transpositions and four-cycles give different orders and counts in $S_5$. The count of k-cycles is $\binom{r}{k}(k-1)!$ in $S_r$; products of disjoint transpositions require division for ordering and pair symmetries. A finite map cannot be surjective when its domain is smaller than its codomain, independently of group structure. Nontriviality and injectivity are distinct properties and should not be confused.',[(r'How many homomorphisms $C_2\to S_3$ exist?',r'The generator may map to an element whose square is identity: the identity or one of three transpositions. Thus there are four homomorphisms.'),(r'Can $C_6$ embed in $S_3$?',r'No. A generator would need a permutation of order 6, but $S_3$ only has element orders 1,2,3. Its size 6 alone does not imply existence of a cyclic subgroup of order 6.')])

add(87,'Abstract Algebra',r'For real $\alpha$, which statements are true?',[r'If $\alpha^3$ is algebraic over $\mathbb Q$, then $\alpha$ is algebraic over $\mathbb Q$',r'$\alpha$ could be algebraic over $\mathbb Q[\sqrt2]$ but not over $\mathbb Q$',r'$\alpha$ need not be algebraic over any subfield of $\mathbb R$',r'There exists a real $\alpha$ not algebraic over $\mathbb Q[\sqrt{-1}]$'],'AD',[r'Algebraicity is transitive through algebraic field extensions.',r'Every element is algebraic over a field containing it, while transcendental real numbers remain transcendental over a finite algebraic extension of Q.'],r'If $\alpha^3$ satisfies nonzero $P(t)\in\mathbb Q[t]$, then $P(\alpha^3)=0$, so the nonzero polynomial $P(x^3)$ proves A. Since $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)/\mathbb Q$ is finite algebraic, any element algebraic over it is algebraic over Q by transitivity; B is false. C is false because $\alpha$ is algebraic over the subfield $\mathbb Q(\alpha)\subseteq\mathbb R$, satisfying $x-\alpha=0$. For D choose any real number transcendental over Q. If it were algebraic over $\mathbb Q(i)$, transitivity through the degree-two extension would make it algebraic over Q, a contradiction. Such real numbers exist because algebraic numbers are countable and the reals uncountable. Thus A,D are true.',r'An element is algebraic over a field K if it is a root of a nonzero polynomial with coefficients in K. Algebraicity is transitive: if L is algebraic over K and an element is algebraic over L, it is algebraic over K. In a finite tower, the degrees multiply, yielding a direct proof. Algebraic powers can also be handled by composing their polynomial relation with the relevant monomial. Every element of K is trivially algebraic over K via a linear polynomial, so assertions denying algebraicity over every containing subfield fail. Transcendence over Q persists after enlarging Q by an algebraic extension. Bracket notation adjoining an algebraic number gives a field here, since every nonzero element has an inverse expressible through the minimal polynomial. Existence of transcendental reals follows from countability of all rational-coefficient polynomials.',[(r'If $\alpha^2=\sqrt2$, is $\alpha$ algebraic over Q?',r'Yes. Squaring again gives $\alpha^4=2$, so $\alpha$ is a root of $x^4-2\in\mathbb Q[x]$.'),(r'Can a real number transcendental over Q be algebraic over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt3)$?',r'No. The intermediate field has finite algebraic degree 2 over Q. Algebraicity over it would imply algebraicity over Q by the tower theorem, contradicting transcendence.')])

add(88,'Number Theory',r'For a prime $p>2019$, how many roots can $f(x)=(x^2-3)(x^2-673)(x^2-2019)$ possibly have in $\mathbb F_p$?',[r'0',r'2',r'3',r'6'],'BD',[r'The three nonzero residues are distinct and $2019=3\cdot673$.',r'Use multiplicativity of the quadratic character.'],r'All three constants are nonzero and distinct modulo p, so root sets of the three quadratics are disjoint. Each factor has either zero or two roots. Let $\epsilon=(3/p)$ and $\delta=(673/p)$ be their quadratic characters. The character of 2019 is $\epsilon\delta$. If both signs are +1, all three factors split and there are six roots. In every other sign pattern exactly one of the three characters is +1, giving two roots. Thus 0 and 3 are impossible, and 2 and 6 are the only possible counts. Both actually occur for primes above 2019: p=2027 gives six roots and p=2029 gives two, as Euler-criterion computations verify. Hence B,D are correct.',r'In a finite field of odd prime order, a nonzero element is either a quadratic residue, with exactly two square roots, or a nonresidue, with none. The Legendre symbol encodes these cases as +1 and $-1$ and is multiplicative. Euler’s criterion computes it as $a^{(p-1)/2}$ modulo p, yielding 1 or $-1$. For a product of quadratic factors, root counts add only if their root sets are disjoint; distinct nonzero constants ensure this here. A multiplicative relation among the constants restricts which sign patterns can occur, so merely listing arbitrary counts 0,2,4,6 would miss the essential dependency. A “possibly” question requires showing that any accepted count is attainable, not just that it survives a bound. Repeated constants or a zero constant would change the count and must be checked against the prime-size premise.',[(r'For odd p, what root counts can $(x^2-a)(x^2-b)(x^2-ab)$ have when a,b,ab are distinct and nonzero?',r'The characters are $\epsilon,\delta,\epsilon\delta$. Either all are positive or exactly one is positive. The counts are therefore 6 or 2, provided the stated distinctness prevents overlap.'),(r'Count roots of $(x^2-1)(x^2-2)(x^2-2)$ in $\mathbb F_7$.',r'The squares modulo 7 include 1 and 2. The first factor has roots $\pm1$ and the second roots $\pm3$; its repetition creates no new distinct roots. The total count is four, showing why disjointness and repeated factors matter.')])

add(89,'Topology',r'Which statements are true?',[r'Every compact topological space is metrizable',r'Every continuous image of a Hausdorff space is Hausdorff',r'If $f:X\to Y$ is continuous and injective and Y is Hausdorff, then X is Hausdorff',r'The intersection of two connected subsets of $\mathbb R$ with its usual topology is empty or connected'],'CD',[r'For C pull back disjoint neighborhoods of the distinct image points.',r'Connected subsets of the real line are intervals; compactness alone does not ensure Hausdorffness.'],r'A is false: a two-point indiscrete space is compact but not Hausdorff, hence not metrizable. B is false: the identity map from a two-point discrete Hausdorff space onto a two-point indiscrete space is continuous, but its image with that topology is not Hausdorff. For C, distinct x,x\prime have distinct images; Hausdorffness of Y supplies disjoint neighborhoods of those images, and their inverse images are disjoint open neighborhoods of x,x\prime. Thus X is Hausdorff. D holds because connected subsets of R are intervals, and an intersection of intervals is either empty or another interval. Hence C,D are correct.',r'A Hausdorff space separates every pair of distinct points by disjoint open neighborhoods. All metric spaces are Hausdorff, but compactness does not imply either Hausdorffness or metrizability. Continuity pulls back open sets; it does not generally push forward enough separating neighborhoods, so a continuous image may lose Hausdorffness. An injective continuous map into a Hausdorff space does inherit separation by pulling neighborhoods back and using distinctness of the images. In the real line, connected sets are exactly intervals, including singletons, because omission of an intermediate point separates a set and the intermediate-value property prevents such a gap. This special order structure makes intersections of connected real subsets connected when nonempty. In general spaces, intersections of connected subsets need not be connected, so the ambient real-line hypothesis in the final statement is essential.',[(r'Why is every metric space Hausdorff?',r'For distinct x,y with distance d>0, the balls of radius d/3 about them are disjoint by the triangle inequality. These are the required separating open neighborhoods.'),(r'Find the intersection of connected sets $[0,2]$ and $(1,3)$ in R.',r'The intersection is $(1,2]$, an interval and therefore connected. It need not be open or closed to be connected.')])

add(90,'Topology',r'Let $X=\mathbb N\cup\{\infty,-\infty\}$. Its open subsets U satisfy either $U\subseteq\mathbb N$ or $X\setminus U$ is finite. Set $A=\mathbb N\cup\{\infty\}$ and $B=\mathbb N\cup\{-\infty\}$. Which sets are compact?',[r'A',r'$X\setminus A$',r'$A\cup B$',r'$A\cap B$'],'ABC',[r'A neighborhood of either added point covers all but finitely many natural numbers.',r'The intersection is the infinite discrete subspace of natural numbers.'],r'A is compact: in any relative open cover, choose one member containing $\infty$. Its defining open set in X is cofinite, so it covers all but finitely many points of A; finitely many further members finish the cover. B has the same property. Option B is the singleton $\{-\infty\}$, compact in every topology. $A\cup B=X$ is compact, either as a finite union of compact subsets or directly by choosing neighborhoods of both added points and covering the finite remainder. But $A\cap B=\mathbb N$ is an infinite discrete subspace, covered by its singletons without a finite subcover. Thus A,B,C are correct and D false. The space is compact but its two added points cannot be separated by disjoint neighborhoods.',r'Compactness means that every open cover has a finite subcover, and it does not require a Hausdorff topology. In a cofinite-neighborhood construction, choosing one cover member containing a special point often leaves only a finite remainder, making compactness immediate. Every finite subset is compact in any topology, and a finite union of compact subsets is compact. Subspace topology must be used when considering a subset: natural-number singletons are open here, so the induced topology on the natural numbers is discrete and an infinite discrete set is not compact. A subset of a compact space need not be compact unless appropriate conditions such as closedness are available. In non-Hausdorff spaces, compact sets need not be closed, and intersecting compact subsets can even give a noncompact set, as this example demonstrates.',[(r'Is $\mathbb N\cup\{\infty\}$ compact when naturals are isolated and infinity has cofinite neighborhoods?',r'Yes. Any open cover has a member containing infinity and hence all but finitely many naturals. One member for each remaining point produces a finite subcover.'),(r'Is an infinite discrete set compact?',r'No. The collection of all singleton open sets covers it, and every finite subcollection covers only finitely many points, so no finite subcover exists.')])
